oximation by setting up a table of values, like the one shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$. We call this the **error.**\n\nFor example, consider the first column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.0$. Substituting $x=1.0$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= x + 1 \\\\\n&= 1.0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2.0 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4x - 3 \\\\\n&= 4(1.0) - 3 \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & &= & 2.0 - 1.0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid 1.0 \\mid \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the first column:\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ | $2.1$ | $2.2$ | $2.3$ | $2.4$ | $2.5$ |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ | $1.4$ | $1.8$ | $2.2$ | $2.6$ | $3.0$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ | $0.7$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFinally, we look for the smallest error. In the bottom row of the table, we see that $0.1$ is the smallest error. This corresponds to $x=1.3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx1.3\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186055953 1064 "Let's consider