action before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186055986 1064 "Sometimes, when calculating probabilities, we have to use our knowledge of permutations. We do this when the order of occurrences in the event matters.\n\nFor instance, suppose we create a $3$-digit number from the digits $1$, $2$, $3$, and $4$, without repetition. What is the probability that our number starts with $1$?\n\nLet $A$ represent the event that our number starts with $1$. We can use the formula\n$$\nP(A) = \\dfrac{n}{N} = \\frac{ {\\textrm{number of ways to choose a } {3} \\textrm{-digit number starting with } {1}}}{ {\\textrm{total number of ways to choose a } {3} \\textrm{-digit number}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of ways to choose a $3$-digit number using the digits $1$, $2$, $3$, and $4$ without repetition. From $4$ digits, we choose $3$ of them, and since the order of the digits matters, we use permutations:\n\n$$\nN = {}_{4}P_3 = \\dfrac{4!}{(4-3)!} = 24\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the number of ways to choose a $3$-digit number starting with $1$. The first 