$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186082910 1074 "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186082961 1074 "To convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2π}{3}) \\cdot (\\frac{180^{∘}}{π}) &= \\frac{2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\cdot π}{3 \\cdot π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^{∘}π}{3π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^{∘}π}{3π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^{∘}}{3} \\\\\n&= 120^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\pi} 3\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $120^\\circ$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186082961 1074 "To convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\pi }{3}} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {180}^{\\circ }}{\\pi }} ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\cdot  {180}^{\\circ } \\cdot  \\pi }{3 \\cdot  \\pi }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {360}^{\\circ } \\pi }{3 \\pi }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {360}^{\\circ } \\cancel{\\pi}}{3 \\canc