 follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 2 \\tan   x \\csc   2 x & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x}{\\cos   x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\color{blue}\\sin   2 x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x}{\\cos   x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\color{blue}2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{2 \\sin   x}}{\\cos   x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cancel{2 \\sin   x} \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\cos }^{2}  x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\sec }^{2}  x\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186087583 1074 "Since $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-2)$, the remainder is $0$. It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= f(2) \\\\\n&= a(2)^{3} + b(2)^{2} - 11(2) - 2 \\\\\n&= 8a + 4b - 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $-8$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)$, it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8 &= f(1) \\\\\n&= a(1)^{3} + b(1)^{2} - 11(1) - 2 \\\\\n&= a + b - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n{0 = 8a + 4b - 24; - 8 = a + b - 13\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n{2a + b = 6; a + b = 5\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b$, we subtract the equations and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\begin{vmatrix}a + b &= 6 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}a + b|= 5 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1$ into\n\n$$\na+b=5\n$$\n\nto find $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, our final solution is\n\n$$\n(a,b) = (1,4)\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186087583 1074 "Since $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-2)$, the remainder is $0$. It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= f(2) \\\\\n&= a(2)^{3} + b(2)^{2} - 11(2) - 2 \\\\\n&= 8a + 4b - 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $-8$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)$, it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8 &= f(1) \\\\\n&= a(1)^{3} + b(1)^{2} - 11(1) - 2 \\\\\n&= a + b - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}0 = 8 a + 4 b - 24 \\\\ - 8 = a + b - 13.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 a + b = 6 \\\\ a + b = 5.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b$, we subtract the equations and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\begin{vmatrix}a + b &= 6 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}a + b|= 5 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1$ into\n\n$$\na+b=5\n$$\n\nto find $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, our final solution is\n\n$$\n(a,b) = (1,4)\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186087651 1074 "Let $T$ be the time it takes for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table:\n\n| Person | Speed (km/hr) | Time (hr) | Distance (km) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Mary** | $5$ | $T$ | $5T$ |\n| **John** | $7$ | $T$ | $7T$ |\n\nAfter $T$ hours, they will have traveled a combined distance of $3\\,\\textrm{km}$. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $3$ and solve for time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5T + 7T &= 3 \\\\\n12T &= 3 \\\\\nT &= \\frac{3}{12} \\\\\nT &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt will take\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nhours for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart. If we convert this time to minutes, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}hr &= \\frac{1}{4}hr \\cdot \\frac{60min}{hr} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}hr \\cdot \\frac{60min}{hr} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 60min \\\\\n&= 15min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $15$ 