 number is twice the other. What are the two numbers?\n\nLet $x$ be the smaller number and $y$ the larger.\n\n- The sum of the two numbers is $30$, so $x+y=30$.\n- One number is twice the other, so $y=2x$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 30 \\\\ y = 2 x\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The second equation $y=2x$ is already solved for $y$, so we can substitute it into the first equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 30 \\\\\nx + 2x &= 30 \\\\\n3x &= 30 \\\\\nx &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=10$ back into the equation $y=2x$ to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x \\\\\n&= 2(10) \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the system is $x=10$ and $y=20$. Therefore, the two numbers are $10$ and $20$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186088855 1064 "Solving a number problem in algebra is a three-step process.\n\n1. First, we name each quantity mentioned in the problem statement with a variable.\n2. Then, we write one or more equations relating the variables.\n3. Finally, we use our knowledge of systems of equations to solve these equations and answer the question.\n\nFor example, suppose the sum of two numbers is $30$. One number is twice the other. What are the two numbers?\n\nLet $x$ be the smaller number and $y$ the larger.\n\n- The sum of the two numbers is $30$, so $x+y=30$.\n- One number is twice the other, so $y=2x$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 30; y = 2x\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The second equation $y=2x$ is already solved for $y$, so we can substitute it into the first equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 30 \\\\\nx + 2x &= 30 \\\\\n3x &= 30 \\\\\nx &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=10$ back into the equation 