tant, yet the denominator is a linear polynomial. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a_n$ is convergent, and it converges to $0$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186097905 1064 "Sequences involving factorials often occur in advanced math. For that reason, we need to know how to analyze them.\n\nLet's consider the sequence $a_n$ given below.\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac {n!}{(n + 1)!},\\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nDoes this sequence converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?\n\nTo evaluate the limit, note the following:\n\n- The trick is to simplify $a_n$ by writing the *larger* factorial in terms of the smaller one.\n- In this case, $(n+1)$! is the larger factorial, and $n$! is the smaller.\n- So, we need to write $(n+1)$! in terms of $n$!\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the definition of the factorial function:\n\n$$\n{n!} = {\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}\n$$\n\nUsing the same definition, we can write $(n+1)$! as\n\n$$\n(n+1)! = \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}}_{n!} \\cdot (n+1)\n$$\n\nReplacing ${\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}$ with $n$! in the above expression, we have\n\n$$\n(n+1)! = n!\\cdot (n+1)\n$$\n\nWe can now use our expression for $(n+1)$! in terms of $n$! to simplify our expression for $a_n$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {a}_{n} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{n !}{( n + 1 ) !}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{n !}{n ! \\cdot  ( n + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{n !}}{\\cancel{n !} \\cdot  ( n + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n + 1}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a_n\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ because the numerator is constant, yet the denominator is a linear polynomial. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a_n$ is convergent, and it converges to $0$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099038 1080 "0.3 \\mathrm{\\% }" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099038 1080 "0.3%" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186099039 1080 "0.2 \\mathrm{\\% }" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099039 1080 "0.2%" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186099040 1080 "0.1 \\mathrm{\\% }" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099040 1080 "0.1%" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186099041 1080 "0.4 \\mathrm{\\% }" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099041 1080 "0.4%" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186099042 1080 "0.0 \\mathrm{\\% }" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186099042 1080 "0.0%" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186100586 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nNotice that the interval $[1,t]$ varies with $t$, so we use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(x) - f(a)}{x-a}\n$$\n\nwith $a=1$ and $x=t$. Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{Δy}{Δx} &= (f(x) - f(a))/(x - a) \\\\\n&= (f(t) - f(1))/(t - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{t^{2} - 1^{2}}{t - 1} \\\\\n&= ((t - 1)(t + 1))/(t - 1) \\\\\n&= ((t - 1)(t + 1))/(t - 1) \\\\\n&= t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186100586 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nNotice that the interval $[1,t]$ varies with $t$, so we use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(x) - f(a)}{x-a}\n$$\n\nwith $a=1$ and $x=t$. Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{\\Delta } y}{\\mathrm{\\Delta } x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( x ) - f ( a )}{x - a}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( t ) - f ( 1 )}