 $N-1$ edges. This leads to an alternative definition of a tree, which also serves as a criterion for verifying whether a given graph is a tree: a tree is a connected graph where the number of edges is one less than the number of vertices.\n\nNotice that the given graph is connected but contains the cycle $v_1-v_2-v_3-v_4-v_5-v_1$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n> The above graph is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{not a tree}}$ because it has $v_{1} - v_{2} - v_{3} - v_{4} - v_{5} - v_{1}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186105509 1074 "Kruskal's algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree of a connected, weighted graph $G$. The pseudocode for Kruskal's algorithm is the following:\n\n> `Initialize a forest containing all the vertices of G and none\n> of its edges`\n> `def Kruskal(): # Kruskal's algorithm`\n> `Order the edges of G by their weight from smallest to largest`\n> `for (e in ordered list of edges): # Go over all edges`\n> `if (ends of e belong to different connected components):`\n> `Add e to the forest`\n\nBy inspection, the order of the edges of the original graph according to their weights is as follows:\n\n$$\nv_{2} - v_{5}, v_{1} - v_{4}, v_{1} - v_{2}, v_{1} - v_{5}, v_{5} - v_{6},; v_{3} - v_{6}, v_{2} - v_{3}, v_{3} - v_{5}, v_{4} - v_{5}\n$$\n\nWe see that $v_2-v_5, v_1-v_4$ and $v_1-v_2$ have already been added to the forest.\n\n$$\nv_{2} - v_{5}, v_{1} - v_{4}, v_{1} - v_{2}, v_{1} - v_{5}, v_{5} - v_{6},; v_{3} - v_{6}, v_{2} - v_{3}, v_{3} - v_{5}, v_{4} - v_{5}\n$$\n\nSo, at the next step of Kruskal's algorithm, we consider the edge with the smallest weight among the remaining ones. This is the edge $v_1 - v_5$. However, adding it to our forest will form a cycle since $v_1$ and $v_5$ belong to the same connected component.\n\n![](images/35/35ca8210d1f144cc2c8025befb4222f01b09f561d2a2d412ac4b8803680451e2.png)\n\nHence, we consider the next edge of the smallest weight: edge $v_5-v_6$. Adding it to our forest will not form a cycle since $