} + (\\frac{2}{0, 1, 1})a^{0}b^{1}c^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2!}{2!0!0!}a^{2} + \\frac{2!}{0!2!0!}b^{2} + \\frac{2!}{0!0!2!}c^{2} + \\frac{2!}{1!1!0!}ab + \\frac{2!}{1!0!1!}ac + \\frac{2!}{0!1!1!}bc \\\\\n&= a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1}) &= (\\frac{4}{2}) = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186111889 1064 "Let's recall the multinomial theorem:\n\n> *For any positive integer $n$, the terms of $(a+b+c)^n$ can be written as*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} a^{k_1} b^{k_2} c^{k_3}$,\n> *where $k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = n$ and the general formula for the multinomial coefficient is*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} = \\dfrac{n!}{k_1!\\, k_2! \\, k_3!}$.\n\nFor example, using the multinomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( a + b + c {)}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{2} {b}^{0} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{2} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{0} {c}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{==} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{1} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{0} {c}^{1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{1} {c}^{1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {a}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {b}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 !}} {c}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} a b + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} a c + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} b c \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {a}^{2} + {b}^{2} + {c}^{2} + 2 a b + 2 a c + 2 b c .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} \\right) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113365 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x - 5 y = 10 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = - 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113365 1080 "{2x - 5y = 10; x =-7" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186113366 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x + 5 y = - 7 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = 10\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113366 1080 "{2x + 5y =-7; x = 10" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186113367 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x - 5 y = - 7 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = 10\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113367 1080 "{2x - 5y =-7; x = 10" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186113368 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x + 5 y = 10 \\\\ \\displaystyle y = - 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113368 1080 "{2x + 5y = 10; y =-7" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186113369 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x - 5 y = 10 \\\\ \\displaystyle y = - 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186113369 1080 "{2x - 5y = 10; y =-7" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186113407 1080 "\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 3 x + 2 y = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = - 2\\end{array} \\right. 