0123c5483bdca87d70384b04494d51b0758b7dc751542b94478a85f.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the incidence matrix of the above graph:\n\n|  | $e_1$ | $e_2$ | $e_3$ | $e_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $v_2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $v_4$ | $0$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $0$ | $1$ |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115024 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = \\cos^2{x} - \\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe are given the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\cos x + \\sin x = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}; \\cos x - \\sin x =-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two equations and using the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cos x + \\sin x)(\\cos x - \\sin x) &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\cos 2x &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115024 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = \\cos^2{x} - \\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe are given the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\cos   x + \\sin   x = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}} \\\\ \\cos   x - \\sin   x = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two equations and using the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cos x + \\sin x)(\\cos x - \\sin x) &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\cos 2x &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115492 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 3 x + 5 y = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle x - 7 y = - 4\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115492 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${3x + 5y = 4; x - 7y =-4$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115493 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = 0\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115493 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${2y = 3; x = 0$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115732 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle y + 3 x - 8 = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle y + 9 = 5\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115732 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${y + 3x - 8 = 2; y + 9 = 5$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115848 1071 "Which of the following lines are vertical asymptotes of the graph of $y = \\sec x$?\n\n1. $x = 0$ $\\frac{π}{2}$\n2. $x = \\frac{π}{2}$\n3. $x = π$ $\\frac{π}{2}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115848 1071 "Which of the following lines are vertical asymptotes of the graph of $y = \\sec x$?\n\n1. $x = 0$ $\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}}}$\n2. $x = \\frac{π}{2}$\n3. $x = π$ $\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}}}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115851 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2}$ and $R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}$?\n\n$$\nA = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{array} \\right] , \\hspace{2em} B = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 2 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right] , \\hspace{2em} C = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array} \\right]\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115851 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2}$ and $R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115927 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n2. $\\sec x = 4$ $\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{3}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186115927 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n2. $\\sec x = 4$ $\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}}$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{3}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115977 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y - 7 x = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle - x + 2 y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186115977 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${2y - 7x = 3; - x + 2y =-1$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186116414 1064 "Cayley's formula for counting the number of labeled trees on $n$ vertices is given by\n\n$$\nT(n) = n^{n-2}\n$$\n\nThere are multiple ways to prove this theorem. Here, we'll use a so-called **double counting** method. We will count the number of *labeled out-trees on $n$ vertices* using two separate methods and equate them, which yields an explicit formula for $T(n)$.\n\n**Method 1 - Rooting and Edge Labeling:**\n\nSuppose we have a tree on $n$ labeled vertices, like the tree shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf02aa3827f40f13c9d4096644064a1375f1cf7f84f807fc914cbdd51b2e448.png)\n\nWe select one of the vertices as the root vertex.\n\n![](images/5a/5a0afb7a3e161f6b45a805321169e63661e6bcc01e9a505f60789cb0d6ac9225.png)\n\nOur selection of the root vertex induces a natural direction of the edges \"away\" from the root vertex. This type of structure is called an **out-tree** and is *unique* for a given tree and choice of root.\n\n![](images/47/4781e78a052b44632d3ef004013eaf2a2d63b2ea73fe8113bebd47ad098512dc.png)\n\nSince there are $n$ vertices in our tree, we have $(n-1)$ edges. Suppose we assign labels $e_1, e_2, \\ldots e_{n-1}$ to these edges. One such configuration is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30b9031552b229fcbe961d4ea1b6af372c33c94596d21fecba6b9dd4becfae37.png)\n\nWe call this configuration a **sequenced out-tree.** Since there are $(n-1)$ edges, we have $(n-1)$! ways of assigning our edge labels.\n\nFor a given tree, we have a choice of $n$ vertices as the root and $(n-1)$! ways of assigning the edge labels. Therefore, the total number of sequenced out-trees for a given tree is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{# sequenced out-trees for a given tree} = n\\cdot (n-1)! = n\n$$\n\nand since there are $T(n)$ possible labeled trees on $n$ vertices, the total number of sequenced out-trees on $n$ vertices equals\n$$\n{\\textrm{# sequenced out-trees on } {n} \\textrm{ vertices}}  = n!\\cdot T(n).\n$$\n\n**Method 2 – Sequential Construction:**\n\nWe now count the number of sequenced out-trees on $n$ vertices using a different method.\n\nSuppose we initiate a rooted forest on $n$ vertices with no edges. Each vertex is a rooted tree, and our forest has $n$ rooted trees.\n\n![](images/9d/9dcf60c61ac002acf1d18866985022d4809d9c6fbf05bd21ff0e400efca00990.png)\n\n$$\ne_1\n$$\n\n$$\ne_2\n$$\n\n$$\ne_3\n$$\n\n![](images/e3/e37d39c83e3d15a504dd6ffdd62301acd2c37ae27a35db4a694faa4b2d0b2f97.png)\n\nSuppose we have already added $n-k$ edges to our tree, which means our forest currently contains $k$ rooted trees.\n\nTo assign the next edge, we must choose a starting vertex (the tail of our directed edge) and an end vertex (the head of our directed edge):\n\n- For the starting vertex, we can choose any of the $n$ vertices on the graph.\n- To end with a sequenced out-tree, we cannot select any vertex in the graph as the end vertex. Instead, we must choose a *root* vertex in *another rooted tree* of our forest. In other words, it must be a root vertex, but it cannot be the root vertex in the same tree as our starting vertex. Once selected, the end vertex is no longer a root vertex.\n\n![](images/cb/cb63ca1f58fa7a8f354c18b686957aedc974c29c4635d84381f9a0acc51f4f14.png)\n\n- Since our graph contains $k$ rooted trees, we have $(k-1)$ choices of edge for a given vertex.\n- Therefore, the number of choices available for the next edge equals $n(k-1)$.\n\nNow, we have $n(k-1)$ choices at every step. Therefore, the total number of directed out-trees equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n# sequenced out-trees on n vertices &= n(n - 1) \\cdot n(n - 2) \\cdot n(n - 3)⋯n \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= n^{n - 1} \\cdot (n - 1)! \\\\\n&= n^{n - 2} \\cdot n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the results from both methods, we have\n\n$$\nn!\\cdot T(n) = n^{n-2} n\n$$\n\nwhich finally yields Cayley's formula\n\n$$\nT(n) = n^{n-2}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186116414 1064 "Cayley's formula for counting the number of labeled trees on $n$ vertices is given by\n\n$$\nT(n) = n^{n-2}\n$$\n\nThere are multiple ways to prove this theorem. Here, we'll use a so-called **double counting** method. We will count the number of *labeled out-trees on $n$ vertices* using two separate methods and equate them, which yields an explicit formula for $T(n)$.\n\n**Method 1 - Rooting and Edge Labeling:**\n\nSuppose we have a tree on $n$ labeled vertices, like the tree shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf02aa3827f40f13c9d4096644064a1375f1cf7f84f807fc914cbdd51b2e448.png)\n\nWe select one of the vertices as the root vertex.\n\n![](images/5a/5a0afb7a3e16