l reachable nodes have been visited.\n\nThe pseudocode for the breadth-first search algorithm is the following:\n\n> `Initialize the empty queue and the list of visited vertices`\n> `def BFS(v): # Breadth-First Search starting from v`\n> `Add the starting vertex v to the queue and to visited`\n> `while (queue is not empty): # Until the queue is not empty`\n> `Pop vertex w from the head of the queue`\n> `for (u adjacent to w): # Go over all neighbors of w`\n> `if (u not in visited):`\n> `Add u to visited`\n> `Add u to the end of the queue`\n\nSo, at the next step of the breadth-first search algorithm, we pop (i.e., remove) the vertex from the head (i.e., the front) of the queue. This would be the vertex $v_1$.\n\nNext, we traverse all the vertices that are adjacent to $v_1$. So, we add $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}{ { {v}_{2}} \\textrm{ and } { {v}_{3}}}}$ to the list of visited vertices and push them into the end of the queue.\n\n| ![](images/7b/7b6efc5ec3aa005203521bf084aad16b544c6dceeeca70f735ce2c0b2ff94e8f.png) | Queue: $v_2$, $v_3$ Visited: $v_1$, $v_2$, $v_3$ |\n| --- | ---: |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186157032 1074 "Breadth-first search (BFS) is an algorithm that systematically explores a graph by starting at a source node and discovering all its directly connected neighbors. Then, it lists and visits these neighbors, layer by layer, until all reachable nodes have been visited.\n\nThe pseudocode for the breadth-first search algorithm is the following:\n\n> `Initialize the empty queue and the list of visited vertices`\n> `def BFS(v): # Breadth-First Search starting from v`\n> `Add the starting vertex v to the queue and to visited`\n> `while (queue is not empty): # Until the queue is not empty`\n> `Pop vertex w from the head of the queue`\n> `for (u adjacent to w): # Go over all neighbors of w`\n> `if (u not in visited):`\n> `Add u to visited`\n> `Add u to the end of the queue`\n\nSo, at the next step of the breadth-first search algorith