592186045436 false] [17592186158436 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've used the fact that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\lambda\n$$\n\nLet's now justify this rule.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then the PMF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{- \\lambda } {\\lambda }^{x}}{x !}} , & x = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 \\ldots  , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x\\in \\{0,1,2\\ldots\\}} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 0 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{0}}{0!} + 1 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{1}}{1!} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{2}}{2!} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{3}}{3!} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}(0 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{0}}{0!} + 1 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{1}}{1!} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{3}}{3!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}(λ + λ^{2} + \\frac{λ^{3}}{2!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= λe^{- λ}(1 + λ + \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + ⋯)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\ne^{\\lambda} = 1+\\lambda + \\dfrac{\\lambda^2}{2!} + \\dfrac{\\lambda^3}{3!} +\\cdots\n$$\n\n(You will learn this when you study Maclaurin series in Calculus). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= λe^{- λ}(1 + λ + \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= λe^{- λ} \\cdot e^{λ} \\\\\n&= λ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nA similar approach could be used to prove the formula for the variance. However, the calculation is more involved, so we omit the details here." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186158436 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've used the fact that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\lambda\n$$\n\nLet's now justify this rule.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then the PMF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{x}}{x!},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0, 1, 2, 3…, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x\\in \\{0,1,2\\ldots\\}} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 0 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{0}}{0!} + 1 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{1}}{1!} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{2}}{2!} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{e^{- λ}λ^{3}}{3!} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}(0 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{0}}{0!} + 1 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{1}}{1!} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{λ^{3}}{3!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}(λ + λ^{2} + \\frac{λ^{3}}{2!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= λe^{- λ}(1 + λ + \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + ⋯)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\ne^{\\lambda} = 1+\\lambda + \\dfrac{\\lambda^2}{2!} + \\dfrac{\\lambda^3}{3!} +\\cdots\n$$\n\n(You will learn this when you study Maclaurin series in Calculus). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= λe^{- λ}(1 + λ + \\frac{λ^{2}}{2!} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= λe^{- λ} \\cdot e^{λ} \\\\\n&= λ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nA similar approach could be used to prove the formula for the variance. However, the calculation is more involved, so we omit the details here." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158437 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we used the fact that if $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac1p\n$$\n\nLet's now justify this result.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then the PMF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}p ( 1 - p {)}^{x - 1} , & x = 1 , 2 , 3 \\ldots  , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x\\in \\{1,2,3\\ldots\\}} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x \\cdot p(1-p)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nWe can factor $p$ out of the summation since it's a constant:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = p \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x (1-p)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nLet $q = 1-p$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = p \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x q^{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, using the generalized binomial theorem, we have that if $\\mid q \\mid < 1$ then\n\n$$\n\\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x q^{x-1} = \\frac{1}{(1-q)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= p \\cdot (1)/((1 - q)^{2}) \\\\\n&= p \\cdot \\frac{1}{p^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186158437 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we used the fact that if $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac1p\n$$\n\nLet's now justify this result.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then the PMF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{p(1 - p)^{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3…, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x\\in \\{1,2,3\\ldots\\}} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x \\cdot p(1-p)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nWe can factor $p$ out of the summation since it's a constant:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = p \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x (1-p)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nLet $q = 1-p$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = p \\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x q^{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, using the generalized binomial theorem, we have that if $\\mid q \\mid < 1$ then\n\n$$\n\\sum_{x=1}^\\infty x q^{x-1} = \\frac{1}{(1-q)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= p \\cdot (1)/((1 - q)^{2}) \\\\\n&= p \\cdot \\frac{1}{p^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158442 1064 "Consider a random experiment with three mutually exclusive and exhaustive events $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$ with\n\n$$\np_i = P(A_i)\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,2,3$. Thus,\n\n$$\np_1 + p_2 + p_3 = 1\n$$\n\nWe repeat the experiment $n$ times independently. Let the random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ be the number of times that events $A_1$, $A_2$, and $A_3$ occur, respectively. Then, we say that the random vector\n\n$$\nX = (X_1, X_2, X_3)\n$$\n\nfollows a **trinomial distribution** with parameters $n$ and\n\n$$\np=(p_1,p_2,p_3)\n$$\n\nand we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Trinomial}(n, (p_1,p_2,p_3))\n$$\n\nThe **trinomial probability mass function** of $X\\sim \\textrm{Trinomial}(n, (p_1,p_2,p_3))$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1,x_2,x_3)=P(X_1=x_1,X_2=x_2,X_3=x_3)=\\dfrac{n!}{x_1!\\,x_2!\\,x_3!}p_1^{x_1}p_2^{x_2}p_3^{x_3}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_i$ are nonnegative integers,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\np_1+p_2+p_3=1\n$$\n\n.\n\nLet's break this down:\n\n- The product $p_1^{x_1}p_2^{x_2}p_3^{x_3}$ gives the probability of getting a specific arrangement.\n- However, there are many ways to get $X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2$ and $X_3 = x_3$. The exact number of possible permutations is given by the trinomial coefficient\n$(n; x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3})$\n\nThe marginal distributions of $X_1$, $X_2$, and $X_3$ are\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim B(n, p_1),\\qquad X_2 \\sim B(n,p_2), \\qquad X_3 \\sim B(n,p_3)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\np_1+p_2+p_3=1\n$$\n\nThis follows from the fact that $X_1$ is the number of \"successes\" in $n$ independent trials with $p_1$ being the probability of \"successes\" in each trial (similarly, for $X_2$ and $X_3$).\n\n**Note:** The random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ are *dependent* because if we multiply their marginal probability mass functions together, we don't get the trinomial probability mass function." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158442 1064 "Consider a random experiment with three mutually exclusive and exhaustive events $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$ with\n\n$$\np_i = P(A_i)\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,2,3$. Thus,\n\n$$\np_1 + p_2 + p_3 = 1\n$$\n\nWe repeat the experiment $n$ times independently. Let the random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ be the number of times that events $A_1$, $A_2$, and $A_3$ occur, respectively. Then, we say that the random vector\n\n$$\nX = (X_1, X_2, X_3)\n$$\n\nfollows a **trinomial distribution** with parameters $n$ and\n\n$$\np=(p_1,p_2,p_3)\n$$\n\nand we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Trinomial}(n, (p_1,p_2,p_3))\n$$\n\nThe **trinomial probability mass function** of $X\\sim \\textrm{Trinomial}(n, (p_1,p_2,p_3))$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1,x_2,x_3)=P(X_1=x_1,X_2=x_2,X_3=x_3)=\\dfrac{n!}{x_1!\\,x_2!\\,x_3!}p_1^{x_1}p_2^{x_2}p_3^{x_3}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_i$ are nonnegative integers,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\np_1+p_2+p_3=1\n$$\n\n.\n\nLet's break this down:\n\n- The product $p_1^{x_1}p_2^{x_2}p_3^{x_3}$ gives the probability of getting a specific arrangement.\n- However, there are many ways to get $X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2$ and $X_3 = x_3$. The exact number of possible permutations is given by the trinomial coefficient\n${\\left( \\begin{array}{c}n \\\\ {x}_{1} , {x}_{2} , {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right)}= \\dfrac{n!}{x_1!\\ x_2!\\ x_3!}.$\n\nThe marginal distributions of $X_1$, $X_2$, and $X_3$ are\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim B(n, p_1),\\qquad X_2 \\sim B(n,p_2), \\qquad X_3 \\sim B(n,p_3)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\np_1+p_2+p_3=1\n$$\n\nThis follows from the fact that $X_1$ is the number of \"successes\" in $n$ independent trials with $p_1$ being the probability of \"successes\" in each trial (similarly, for $X_2$ and $X_3$).\n\n**Note:** The random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ are *dependent* because if we multiply their marginal probability mass functions together, we don't get the trinomial probability mass function." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186158444 1064 "![](images/9b/9b4292d1c6f6f7106430e5f52ae7c60be6db3be4852666838f22aa5320c184ef.png)\n\nLet's apply Dijkstra's algorithm to the graph above, starting at $v_1$.\n\n| **Step 1.** |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| ![](images/22/22c5028207af526fd0e7eb3127f407430c3c4b1e01412a31410594a77b17ad37.png) | Add all vertices to the priority queue with $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}v_1}$ at the head, set the distance(s) to this vertex equal to $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$, and set no previous vertex.<br>Priority Queue: $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4, v_5, v_6}$ $v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_