3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}v_6}$$v_2$$v_1$ |\n\n| **Step 7.** |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| ![](images/00/0013ec8fa6db9f5b1e1c35ce9905d2c44b9be8d8f3fcd85d296fe09b987eab61.png) | Pop $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}v_4}$ from the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{head}}$ of the priority queue. Since none of its neighbors are in the queue, we do nothing. Finally, the priority queue is empty.<br>Priority Queue:$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_6$Distances$0$$5$$10$$11$$7$$7$Previous$-1$$v_1$$v_5$$v_6$$v_2$$v_1$ |\n\nThe results show that the distance from $v_1$ to $v_4$ is $11$ (highlighted in the table below).\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ | $v_6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Distances | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}11}$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| Previous | $-1$ | $v_1$ | $v_5$ | $v_6$ | $v_2$ | $v_1$ |\n\nTo find the path from $v_1$ to $v_4$, we trace back through the previous vertices: the previous vertex of $v_4$ is $v_6$, whose previous vertex is $v_1$.\n\nTherefore, a shortest path from $v_1$ to $v_4$ is $v_1 - v_6 - v_4$.\n\n![](images/df/df73d901c21d6017c54cc8ed8d6824f9284b61f872ef066b684de06e30de415c.png)" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158483 1064 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers $n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(n_i,n_j)=1\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\nthen for any integers $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  {a}_{1} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{2} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{2} ) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} \\vdots  &  \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{k} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{k} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo $n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the smallest natural number solution to the following system of linear congruences:\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  4 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  7 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 9 )\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThis system can be interpreted as follows. Suppose we have $x$ identical \"bricks\" and we know that:\n\n- if we stack them in piles with $5$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $4$ bricks.\n- if we stack them in piles with $9$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $7$ bricks.\n\nHow can we find the smallest possible number $x$?\n\n![](images/dd/dd89c8b56cd455b7aafa41cd8d305a7455246141851c49d26ba5cf955f87de7a.png)\n\nFirst, we note that $5$ and $9$ are coprime. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 9 = 45\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 4 + 5t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 4 + 5t & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 5t & ≡3 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $9$ is a relatively small modulus, we can find the multiplicative inverse of $5$ by trial and error. We find that $2 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:9)$. So, we multiply both sides of the above congruence by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ≡3 & (mod9) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5t & ≡2 \\cdot 3 & (mod9) \\\\ t & ≡6 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 6 + 9s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4 + 5t \\\\\n&= 4 + 5(6 + 9s) \\\\\n&= 34 + 45s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 34 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:45)$ is the unique solution modulo $45$. The solution $x=34$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system, and all other solutions are obtained by adding or subtracting multiples of $45$ to this solution.\n\nFinally, let's check that this is indeed correct. Substituting $x=34$ into the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 34 - 6(5) & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 4 & ≡4 & (mod5), ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=34$ into the second congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 34 - 3(9) & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 7 & ≡7 & (mod9).✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186158483 1064 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers $n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(n_i,n_j)=1\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\nthen for any integers $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k$ the system\n\n$$\n{x≡a_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(modn_{1}) \\\\ x≡a_{2}\\end{vmatrix}(modn_{2}); ⋮; x≡a_{k} \\mid (modn_{k})\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo $n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the smallest natural number solution to the following system of linear congruences:\n${x≡4\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ x≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod9)$\n\nThis system can be interpreted as follows. Suppose we have $x$ identical \"bricks\" and we know that:\n\n- if we stack them in piles with $5$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $4$ bricks.\n- if we stack them in piles with $9$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $7$ bricks.\n\nHow can we find the smallest possible number $x$?\n\n![](images/dd/dd89c8b56cd455b7aafa41cd8d305a7455246141851c49d26ba5cf955f87de7a.png)\n\nFirst, we note that $5$ and $9$ are coprime. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 9 = 45\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 4 + 5t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 4 + 5t & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 5t & ≡3 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $9$ is a relatively small modulus, we can find the multiplicative inverse of $5$ by trial and error. We find that $2 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:9)$. So, we multiply both sides of the above congruence by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ≡3 & (mod9) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5t & ≡2 \\cdot 3 & (mod9) \\\\ t & ≡6 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 6 + 9s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4 + 5t \\\\\n&= 4 + 5(6 + 9s) \\\\\n&= 34 + 45s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 34 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:45)$ is the unique solution modulo $45$. The solution $x=34$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system, and all other solutions are obtained by adding or subtracting multiples of $45$ to this solution.\n\nFinally, let's check that this is indeed correct. Substituting $x=34$ into the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 34 - 6(5) & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 4 & ≡4 & (mod5), ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=34$ into the second congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 34 - 3(9) & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 7 & ≡7 & (mod9).✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158495 1064 "When the number of observations $n$ of a binomial random variable is large, it can be difficult to calculate binomial probabilities due to the extremely large numbers involved. However, provided that some constraints are satisfied, we can closely approximate a binomial distribution as a Poisson distribution.\n\nFor example, suppose that at a lightbulb factory, $0.5\\%$ of lightbulbs are defective. If a company manufactures $1\\,000$ lightbulbs, what is the probability that $10$ of them will be defective?\n\nLet $X$ be the number of defective lightbulbs. If there are $n=1\\,000$ lightbulbs and the probability of any single lightbulb being defective is\n\n$$\np=0.5\\% = 0.005\n$$\n\nthen we can calculate\n\n$$\nP(X=10)\n$$\n\nusing the probability mass function for the binomial distribution, as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X = 10) = (\\frac{1000}{10})(0.005)^{10}(0.995)^{990}\n$$\n\nHowever, this product is difficult to compute because\n\n- the binomial coefficient is huge:\n$\\binom{1\\,000}{10} = \\dfrac{1\\,000!}{990! \\cdot 10!} = 263\\,409\\,560\\,461\\,970\\,212\\,832\\,400$\n- the power $(0.005)^{10}$ is extremely small:\n$(0.005)^{10} = 0.000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,009\\,765\\,625$\n\nThankfully, it turns out that when $n$ is large, and $p$ is small, we can approximate a binomial distribution using a Poisson distribution with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = np\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = np = (1\\,000)(0.005) = 5\n$$\n\nUsing the Poisson approximation, the calculation becomes much more tractable. We get\n\n$$\nP(X = 10) \\mid \\approx \\frac{5^{10}e^{-5}}{10!}; \\approx \\frac{9765625e^{-5}}{3628800}; \\approx 0.018133\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nThis is indeed very close to the binomial probability, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 10) &= (\\frac{1000}{10})(0.005)^{10}(0.995)^{990} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.017996\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** There's not a single broadly accepted rule of thumb for using the Poisson approximation to the binomial distribution. Here, we will use the rule of thumb to use the Poisson approximation when $n>20$ and $p<0.05$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158495 1064 "When the number of observations $n$ of a binomial random variable is large, it can be difficult to calculate binomial probabilities due to the extremely large numbers involved. However, provided that some constraints are satisfied, we can closely approximate a binomial distribution as a Poisson distribution.\n\nFor example, suppose that at a lightbulb factory, $0.5\\%$ of lightbulbs are defective. If a company manufactures $1\\,000$ lightbulbs, what is the probability that $10$ of them will be defective?\n\nLet $X$ be the number of defective lightbulbs. If there are $n=1\\,000$ lightbulbs and the probability of any single lightbulb being defective is\n\n$$\np=0.5\\% = 0.005\n$$\n\nthen we can calculate\n\n$$\nP(X=10)\n$$\n\nusing the probability mass function for the binomial distribution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle P ( X = 10 ) = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{10} \\right) ( 0.005 {)}^{10} ( 0.995 {)}^{990}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHowever, this product is difficult to compute because\n\n- the binomial coefficient is huge:\n$\\binom{1\\,000}{10} = \\dfrac{1\\,000!}{990! \\cdot 10!} = 263\\,409\\,560\\,461\\,970\\,212\\,832\\,400$\n- the power $(0.005)^{10}$ is extremely small:\n$(0.005)^{10} = 0.000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,009\\,765\\,625$\n\nThankfully, it turns out that when $n$ is large, and $p$ is small, we can approximate a binomial distribution using a Poisson distribution with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = np\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = np = (1\\,000)(0.005) = 5\n$$\n\nUsing the Poisson approximation, the calculation becomes much more tractable. We get\n\n$$\nP(X = 10) \\mid \\approx \\frac{5^{10}e^{-5}}{10!}; \\approx \\frac{9765625e^{-5}}{3628800}; \\approx 0.018133\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nThis is indeed very close to the binomial probability, which is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 10 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{10} \\right) ( 0.005 {)}^{10} ( 0.995 {)}^{990} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  0.017996\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** There's not a single broadly accepted rule of thumb for using the Poisson approximation to the binomial distribution. Here, we will use the rule of thumb to use the Poisson approximation when $n>20$ and $p<0.05$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186158549 1064 "Suppose there are $3$ red, $2$ green, and $5$ blue balls in a box. Let's consider a sequence of $5$ independent draws of one ball from the box *with replacement* (i.e., we put the ball back in the box after drawing it). So, on each draw, we have\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{red}) = \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\qquad P(\\textrm{green}) = \\dfrac{2}{10}, \\qquad P(\\textrm{blue}) = \\dfrac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nSuppose we draw $5$ balls and get the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{red},\\qquad \\textrm{green}, \\qquad \\textrm{green}, \\qquad \\textrm{blue}, \\qquad \\textrm{blue}\n$$\n\nThe probability of getting precisely this sequence of balls is\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right)^{1}\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2}{10}\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{5}{10}\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we were interested in finding the probability of getting one red, two green, and two blue balls *in any order*. In other words, we wish to calculate the joint probability\n\n$$\nP(X_1 = 1, X_2 = 2, X_3 = 2)\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1$, $X_2$, and $X_3$ are the numbers of red, green, and blue balls drawn from the box, respectively. To find this probability, we must multiply the probability we found earlier by the number of possible permutations of $1$ red, $2$ green, and $2$ blue balls.\n\nThe exact number of possible permutations is given by the trinomial coefficient\n$(5; 1, 2, 2)$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting $1$ red, $2$ green, and $2$ blue balls *in any order* is\n\n$$\nP(X_1 = 1, X_2 = 2, X_3 = 2) = \\dfrac{5!}{1!\\ 2!\\ 2!} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right)^{1}\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2}{10}\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{5}{10}\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nThe tuple\n\n$$\nX = (X_1, X_2, X_3)\n$$\n\nis an example of a **trinomial random variable**." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186158549 1064 "Suppose there are $3$ red, $2$ green, and $5$ blue balls in a box. Let's consider a sequence of $5$ independent draws of one ball from the box *with replacement* (i.e., we put the ball back in the box after drawing it). So, on each draw, we have\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{red}) = \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\qquad P(\\textrm{green})