gned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^(^)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{N - 1}∑O_{i} \\cdot (y_{i}^{mid} - μ^(^))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{40 - 1}(8 \\cdot (2 - 5.05)^{2} + 12 \\cdot (4 - 5.05)^{2} \\\\\n&= + 11 \\cdot (6 - 5.05)^{2} + 9 \\cdot (8 - 5.05)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4.51025… \\\\\n&\\approx 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen conducting a chi-square test, we lose a degree of freedom for every estimated parameter:\n\n- If *only one* parameter must be estimated, then we have two constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 2$.\n- If *both* parameters must be estimated, then we have three constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 3$.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186163180 1064 "Let's remind ourselves how to estimate $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ from sample data.\n\nSuppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 3$ | $8$ |\n| $3\\leq y_i < 5$ | $12$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 7$ | $11$ |\n| $7\\leq y_i < 9$ | $9$ |\n\nSuppose we wish to test the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n\nWe're not given the mean $\\mu$ nor the variance $\\sigma^2$, so we have to estimate them from the sample data.\n\n- For the mean, we have the following estimate:\n$μ^(^)|= \\frac{weighted sum of the midpoints}{total number of observations}; = \\frac{1}{N}∑_(i)O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid}$\n- For the variance, we have\n$\\widehat{\\sigma}^2 = \\dfrac{1}{N-1}\\sum_i O_i \\cdot (y_i^{\\textrm{mid}} - \\widehat{\\mu})^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\mu}$ is our estimate for the mean $\\mu$, $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ is our estimate for the variance $\\sigma^2$,$N$ is the total number of observations, $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ is the midpoint of an observational class $(a_i, b_i)$.\n\nLet's compute estimates for the mean and variance in this case:\n\nThe total number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 8+12+11+9= 40\n$$\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of each observational class.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ | $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 3$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $3\\leq y_i < 5$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 7$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n| $7\\leq y_i < 9$ | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nOur estimate $\\widehat{\\mu}$ for the mean $\\mu$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^) &= \\frac{1}{N}∑O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{40}(8 \\cdot 2 + 12 \\cdot 4 + 11 \\cdot 6 + 9 \\cdot 8) \\\\\n&= 5.05\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{N - 1}} \\sum  {O}_{i} \\cdot  ( {y}_{i}^{\\text{mid}} - \\hat{\\mu } {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{40 - 1}} ( 8 \\cdot  ( 2 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 12 \\cdot  ( 4 - 5.05 {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} + 11 \\cdot  ( 6 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 9 \\cdot  ( 8 - 5.05 {)}^{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.51025 \\ldots  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}4.5}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWhen conducting a chi-square test, we lose a degree of freedom for every estimated parameter:\n\n- If *only one* parameter must be estimated, then we have two constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 2$.\n- If *both* parameters must be estimated, then we have three constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 3$.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186163197 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to conduct chi-square goodness-of-fit tests to determine whether some sample data fit a continuous uniform distribution.\n\nFirst, let's remind ourselves of some basic facts.\n\n- Given a random variable $X \\sim U[\\alpha, \\beta]$, the probability density function (PDF) of $X$ is given by\n$f(x) =\n    {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\beta  - \\alpha }} , \\hspace{2em} & a \\le  x \\le  b , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n- For any interval $[x_1, x_2] \\subseteq [\\alpha, \\beta]$, the probability that $X$ lies within that interval is\n$P(x_1 \\leq X \\leq x_2) = \\displaystyle \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} f(x) \\, dx = \\dfrac{x_2 - x_1}{\\beta-\\alpha}$.\n- If we divide the range $[\\alpha, \\beta]$ into $k$ intervals, then the expected frequency of observations falling in the $i$th interval $[x_{i-1}, x_i]$ is given by\n$E_i = N \\cdot P(x_{i-1} \\leq X \\leq x_i) = N \\cdot \\dfrac{x_i - x_{i-1}}{\\beta-\\alpha}$.\n\nLet's get some practice at computing expected frequencies using the continuous uniform distribution." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186163197 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to conduct chi-square goodness-of-fit tests to determine whether some sample data fit a continuous uniform distribution.\n\nFirst, let's remind ourselves of some basic facts.\n\n- Given a random variable $X \\sim U[\\alpha, \\beta]$, the probability density function (PDF) of $X$ is given by\n${\\frac{1}{β - α},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n- For any interval $[x_1, x_2] \\subseteq [\\alpha, \\beta]$, the probability that $X$ lies within that interval is\n$P(x_1 \\leq X \\leq x_2) = \\displaystyle \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} f(x) \\, dx = \\dfrac{x_2 - x_1}{\\beta-\\alpha}$.\n- If we divide the range $[\\alpha, \\beta]$ into $k$ intervals, then the expected frequency of observations falling in the $i$th interval $[x_{i-1}, x_i]$ is given by\n$E_i = N \\cdot P(x_{i-1} \\leq X \\leq x_i) = N \\cdot \\dfrac{x_i - x_{i-1}}{\\beta-\\alpha}$.\n\nLet's get some practice at computing expected frequencies using the continuous uniform distribution." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186163206 1064 "Suppose a bag contains $10$ candies, $4$ of which are blue and $6$ are orange. We randomly select $3$ candies from the bag *without replacement* and count the number of blue candies.\n\nLet's assume we run this experiment and draw the following sequence:\n$\\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}},\\qquad {\\color{black}{\\textrm{orange}}},\\qquad \\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}}$\n\nA diagram like the one shown below can help us visualize this process. It helps to imagine the bag containing two distinct sets of candies (blue and orange).\n\n![](images/c3/c3d4a81213cdbabeacca7e392ca2e2234df3f10c5682c7de8f5edeaa05de03cf.png)\n\nLet's interpret choosing a blue candy as a \"success\" and orange as a \"failure.\"\n\nLet the random variable $X$ equal the number of successes (blue candies in the sample). In the example above, we have $X=2$. The probability\n\n$$\nP(X=2)\n$$\n\nis given by the following ratio:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nThe denominator represents all possible ways to choose $3$ candies from $10$, while the numerator represents only those cases where exactly $2$ are blue.\n\nTo calculate this probability, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the number of samples of size $3$ containing $2$ blue candies:The number of ways of selecting $2$ blue candies from a total of $4$ in the population is given by\n$\\displaystyle {}_{4}C_{2} = \\binom{4}{2}$.\nThe number of ways of selecting $1$ orange candy from a total of $6$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{6}C_{1} = \\binom{6}{1}$.\nSo, by the *rule of product,* the number of samples of size $3$ with precisely $2$ blue candies is\n$# of ways of selecting [math]2[/math] blue candies \\cdot # of ways of selecting [math]1[/math] orange candy; = (\\frac{4}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{6}{1})$.\n(Note. The symbol $\\textrm{#}$ is shorthand for \"number of\").\n- **Step 2**: Determine the total number of samples of size $3$.This is simply the number of ways of selecting $3$ objects from a population of $10$. This is given by\n${}_{10}C_{3} = \\binom{10}{3}$.\n- **Step 3**: Divide the results.Therefore, we conclude that\n$P(X = 2)|= \\frac{# of samples of size [math]3[/math] with precisely [math]2[/math] blue candies}{# of samples of size [math]3[/math]}; = ((\\frac{4}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{6}{1}))/((\\frac{10}{3})); = \\frac{3}{10}$.\n\nThis same reasoning applies in more general situations where we sample without replacement. If we have a population of $N$ objects, where $K$ are \"successes,\" and we randomly draw $n$ objects, then the number of successes follows a so-called **hypergeometric distribution,** and we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(N, K, n)\n$$\n\nIn our example, we had\n$X\\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(10, 4, 3)$,\nsince there were $N=10$ candies, $K=4$ blue candies (successes), and we were considering samples of size $n=3$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186163206 1064 "Suppose a bag contains $10$ candies, $4$ of which are blue and $6$ are orange. We randomly select $3$ candies from the bag *without replacement* and count the number of blue candies.\n\nLet's assume we run this experiment and draw the following sequence:\n$\\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}},\\qquad {\\color{black}{\\textrm{orange}}},\\qquad \\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}}$\n\nA diagram like the one shown below can help us visualize this process. It helps to imagine the bag containing two distinct sets of candies (blue and orange).\n\n![](images/c3/c3d4a81213cdbabeacca7e392ca2e2234df3f10c5682c7de8f5edeaa05de03cf.png)\n\nLet's interpret choosing a blue candy as a \"success\" and orange as a \"failure.\"\n\nLet the random variable $X$ equal the number of successes (blue candies in the sample). In the example above, we have $X=2$. The probability\n\n$$\nP(X=2)\n$$\n\nis given by t