ow of the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186176586 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = xe^{y}, y(2) = 0$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186176586 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = xe^{y}, y(2) = 0$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186176956 1064 "The table below gives the moment-generating functions $M(t)$ of some common continuous probability distributions.\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a,b]$ | ${(e^{tb} - e^{ta})/(t(b - a)),\\begin{vmatrix}t \\ne 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t = 0$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ | $\\exp\\left({\\mu t+\\dfrac12\\sigma^2t^2}\\right)$ | $\\,\\,\\,t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\,\\,$ |\n| $\\chi^2(k)$ | $(1-2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t<\\dfrac12$ |\n| $\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ | $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha,\\lambda)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}\\right)^\\alpha$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n\nFrom here, we can make some observations:\n\n- At the beginning of this lesson, we proved the formula for a continuous uniform random variable $X\\sim U[a,b]$ for some specific values of $a$ and $b$, where we assumed $t\\neq 0$. However, when $t=0$, we have\n$M(0)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot e^{0 \\cdot x}dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a}dx; = \\frac{b - a}{b - a}; = 1$.\n\n- Notice the similarity between the MGFs of the exponential and gamma random variables. This is reflective of the relationship between the distributions: $\\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ gives the total time until the next event occurs in a Poisson process, while $\\Gamma(\\alpha, \\lambda)$ gives the total time for $\\alpha$ events to occur. In a future lesson, we will discuss the precise relationship between these two MGFs." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186176956 1064 "The table below gives the moment-generating functions $M(t)$ of some common continuous probability distributions.\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a,b]$ | ${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ | $\\exp\\left({\\mu t+\\dfrac12\\sigma^2t^2}\\right)$ | $\\,\\,\\,t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\,\\,$ |\n| $\\chi^2(k)$ | $(1-2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t<\\dfrac12$ |\n| $\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ | $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha,\\lambda)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}\\right)^\\alpha$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n\nFrom here, we can make some observations:\n\n- At the beginning of this lesson, we proved the formula for a continuous uniform random variable $X\\sim U[a,b]$ for some specific values of $a$ and $b$, where we assumed $t\\neq 0$. However, when $t=0$, we have\n$M(0)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot e^{0 \\cdot x}dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a}dx; = \\frac{b - a}{b - a}; = 1$.\n\n- Notice the similarity between the MGFs of the exponential and gamma random variables. This is reflective of the relationship between the distributions: $\\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ gives the total time until the next event occurs in a Poisson process, while $\\Gamma(\\alpha, \\lambda)$ gives the total time for $\\alpha$ events to occur. In a future lesson, we will discuss the precise relationship between these two MGFs." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186177007 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable. We know that the variance of $X$ can be expressed in terms of the raw moments $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nLet $M(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E [X] = M'(0), \\qquad \\textrm E [X^2] = M''(0)\n$$\n\nwe can express the variance of $X$ in terms of derivatives of the MGF evaluated at zero:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =M''(0) - [M'(0)]^2\n$$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThen, the MGF is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= f(0)e^{0t} + f(1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the first derivative and the second derivative of MGF, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}, \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(M^{′}(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= M^{″}(0) - (M^{′}(0))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186177007 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable. We know that the variance of $X$ can be expressed in terms of the raw moments $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nLet $M(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E [X] = M'(0), \\qquad \\textrm E [X^2] = M''(0)\n$$\n\nwe can express the variance of $X$ in terms of derivatives of the MGF evaluated at zero:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =M''(0) - [M'(0)]^2\n$$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThen, the MGF is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= f(0)e^{0t} + f(1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the first derivative and the second derivative of MGF, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}, \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(M^{′}(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= M^{″}(0) - (M^{′}(0))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186177020 1064 "Suppose that Alice has a $20\\%$ chance of being late for work if it rains and a $5\\%$ chance of being late for work if it doesn't. Where Alice lives, the probability that it rains on a randomly selected day is $10\\%$.\n\nAlice's boss wants to determine the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day.\n\nOne way of modeling this situation is to use a tree diagram.\n\nBefore we draw our tree diagram, let's summarize what we know:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{Rain}) = 0.1, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid Rain}) = 0.2, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid No Rain}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nWe're given probability data on Alice being late *given* whether or not it rains, which suggests that we set up our tree diagram as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af417611214953e21920827706eb266e8c069414271980120f3f70a60e95fb5.png)\n\nWe now fill in the missing probabilities:\n\n- For the missing probability on the first level of our diagram, we have\n$P(No Rain)|= 1 - P(Rain); = 1 - 0.1; = 0.9$.\n- For the missing probabilities on the second level, we have\n$P(Not Late \\mid Rain)|= 1 - P(Late \\mid Rain); = 1 - 0.2; = 0.8$,\n$P(Not Late \\mid No Rain)|= 1 - P(Late \\mid No Rain); = 1 - 0.05; = 0.95$.\n\nWe add these probabilities to our diagram.\n\n![](images/f2/f2a896e33e582a384d8ed3f21a1b55f6aaf96641154c55e93d83f344122613af.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day, we first highlight the relevant branches.\n\n![](images/ea/ead309fdf29fcf57a8d1ab6911650ac3cdc551207a9934d9c655469121fbdfd0.png)\n\nRemember that we multiply *across* the branches and add *between* them. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Late) &= P(Rain) \\cdot P(Late \\mid Rain) + P(No Rain) \\cdot P(Late \\mid No Rain) \\\\\n&= (0.1)(0.2) + (0.9)(0.05) \\\\\n&= 0.065 \\\\\n&= 6.5%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $6.5\\%$ chance that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186177020 1064 "Suppose that Alice has a $20\\%$ chance of being late for work if it rains and a $5\\%$ chance of being late for work if it doesn't. Where Alice lives, the probability that it rains on a randomly selected day is $10\\%$.\n\nAlice's boss wants to determine the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day.\n\nOne way of modeling this situation is to use a tree diagram.\n\nBefore we draw our tree diagram, let's summarize what we know:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{Rain}) = 0.1, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid Rain}) = 0.2, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid No Rain}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nWe're given probability data on Alice being late *given* whether or not it rains, which suggests that we set up our tree diagram as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af417611214953e21920827706eb266e8c069414271980120f3f70a60e95fb5.png)\n\nWe now fill in the missi