eter. Notice that the meters unit $(\\textrm m)$ canceled away, leaving only the units we're interested in $(\\textrm{cm}$ and $\\textrm{km})$.\n\nTo convert $0.006\\,\\textrm{km}$ into centimeters, we multiply by the conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\text{km}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{km}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 600 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.006\\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $600\\,\\textrm{cm}$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186189426 1064 "One way to compare fractions with no common numerators or denominators is to compare both fractions to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAs an example, let's fill in the empty box in the comparison statement below:\n\n$$\n00\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ is *less than* $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ because its numerator is less than one-half of its denominator.\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ is *greater than* $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ because its numerator is greater than one-half of its denominator.\n- Since $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ is less than one-half yet $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ is greater than one-half, we conclude that $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ is less than $\\dfrac{5}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8} < \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result using a fraction model:\n\n![](images/b0/b0f44c3a73db1de54641b14cacc4d2a3b73c4ea78896956c935aef4d2e8ab4ae.png)\n\nDetermining whether a fraction is greater than or less than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2