ject the null hypothesis, because\n\n$$\nP(X \\le 0) \\approx 0.1% < α, P(X \\le 1) \\approx 1.1% < α, P(X \\le 2) \\approx 4.4% < α\n$$\n\nAll of these values lie in the critical region. However, $X=3$ does not lie in the critical region because\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 3) \\approx 11.72\\% > \\alpha\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical region for this hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\nX \\in \\{0, 1, {\\color{red}{2}}\\}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this as\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nThe value\n\n$$\nX={\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nis called the **critical value**. The critical value lies on the boundary between the critical region and the region where we do not reject $H_0$.\n\nFinally, note that the critical region and critical value depend on our choice of $\\alpha{:}$\n\n- If we set $\\alpha = 1\\%$, for example, this reduces the likelihood of rejecting the null hypothesis.\n- Consequently, the critical region is now $X\\in \\{{\\color{red}{0}}\\}$ (or $X\\leq 0$), and the critical value is $X={\\color{red}{0}}$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186199459 1064 "Suppose we have a coin that we suspect is biased *against* landing on heads (i.e., the coin is *less* likely to land on heads than tails). We wish to design a hypothesis test at the\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 5\\%\n$$\n\nsignificance level to determine whether or not our suspicions are correct with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:\\: p =\\dfrac12$\n- $H_1:\\:\\: p <\\dfrac12$\n\nLet the test statistic $X$ equal the number of heads we get when the coin is tossed at random $10$ times in a row. Under the null hypothesis, our test statistic $X$ can be modeled as the following binomial random variable:\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/de/deba0d0521b5ec44a22c1964f6d2541d72b78080ff8265cccc90d19b853e254f.png)\n\nThe **critical region** is a set containing all possible $X$-values that are collectively unlikely under the null hypothesis and, therefore, cause the null hypothesis to be rejected. For *left-tailed* hypothesis tests like this, the critical regions lie to the *left* of the corresponding probability distribution.\n\nNotice that getting either $0$, $1$, or $2$ heads out of $10$ coin tosses causes us to reject the null hypothesis, because\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  0 ) \\approx  0.1 \\mathrm{\\% } < \\alpha  , \\hspace{2em} P ( X \\le  1 ) \\approx  1.1 \\mathrm{\\% } < \\alpha  , \\hspace{2em} P ( X \\le  2 ) \\approx  4.4 \\mathrm{\\% } < \\alpha  .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nAll of these values lie in the critical region. However, $X=3$ does not lie in the critical region because\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 3) \\approx 11.72\\% > \\alpha\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical region for this hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\nX \\in \\{0, 1, {\\color{red}{2}}\\}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this as\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nThe value\n\n$$\nX={\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nis called the **critical value**. The critical value lies on the boundary between the critical region and the region where we do not reject $H_0$.\n\nFinally, note that the critical region and critical value depend on our choice of $\\alpha{:}$\n\n- If we set $\\alpha = 1\\%$, for example, this reduces the likelihood of rejecting the null hypothesis.\n- Consequently, the critical region is now $X\\in \\{{\\color{red}{0}}\\}$ (or $X\\leq 0$), and the critical value is $X={\\color{red}{0}}$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186199531 1064 "Recall that a statement $\\beta$ *follows* from statements $\\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\ldots, \\alpha_n$ if $\\beta$ is true whenever *all* of $\\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\ldots, \\alpha_n$ are true. This is called a *logical inference*.\n\nLet's consider another logical inference rule known as **disjunctive syllogism** (**disjunction elimination** or simply **elimination**). We can state this rule as follows:\n\n$$\nP∨Q; ¬P; ∴Q\n$$