 $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nBoth $P \\lor Q$ and $\\lnot P$ have the truth value ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ (true) only in the third row of the table. For this row, $Q$ is also ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ (true). So, according to the definition, we have a logical inference.\n\nSince disjunction is commutative and the order of the premises does not matter, we have the following variants of the rule:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{l}P \\lor  Q \\\\ \\mathrm{\\neg } P \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} Q\\end{array}}\n\\qquad\\qquad\n\n{\\begin{array}{l}P \\lor  Q \\\\ \\mathrm{\\neg } Q \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} P\\end{array}}\n\\qquad\\qquad\n\n{\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{\\neg } P \\\\ P \\lor  Q \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} Q\\end{array}}\n\\qquad\\qquad\n\n{\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{\\neg } Q \\\\ P \\lor  Q \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} P\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186199532 1064 "Let's now consider another logical inference rule called **denying the consequent** (or **modus tollens** in Latin):\n\n$$\nP⇒Q; ¬Q; ∴¬P\n$$\n\nIt reads as follows:\n\n> *We have $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and $\\lnot Q$. Therefore, $\\lnot P$.*\n\nWe can use a truth table to check that the rule is valid.\n\n|  |  |  | Premise 1 | Premise 2 | Conclusion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P$ | $Q$ |  | $P \\Rightarrow Q$ | $\\lnot Q$ | $\\lnot P$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ |\n\nBoth $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and $\\lnot Q$ have 