y of implication** (**chain argument** or **hypothetical syllogism**):\n\n$$\nP⇒Q; Q⇒R; ∴P⇒R\n$$\n\nThis rule is read as follows:\n\n> *We have $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q \\Rightarrow R$. Therefore, $P \\Rightarrow R$.*\n\nWe can use a truth table to check this rule is valid:\n\n|  |  |  |  | Premise 1 | Premise 2 | Conclusion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P$ | $Q$ | $R$ |  | $P \\Rightarrow Q$ | $Q \\Rightarrow R$ | $P \\Rightarrow R$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ |\n\nBoth $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q \\Rightarrow R$ have the truth value ${\\color{blue}\\mathrm{T}}$ (true) in the first, fifth, seventh, and eighth rows of the table only. For these rows, $P \\Rightarrow R$ also has the truth value ${\\color{red}\\mathrm{T}}$ (true). So, according to the definition, we have a logical inference.\n\nSince the order of the premises does not matter, we have the following variants of the rule:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{l}P \\Rightarrow  Q \\\\ Q \\Rightarrow  R \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} P \\Rightarrow  R\\end{array}}\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qqu