\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe can't \"factor out\" the quantifier since, in general,\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x) \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\not\\equiv}\\exists x \\, (P(x) \\land Q(x))\n$$\n\nHowever, there is a workaround. If a variable is bound, we can't substitute anything into it. So, we can change any such variables in one of the predicates to some other variable. For example, let's change $x$ to $y$ in the second predicate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x) \\equiv \\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}y} \\, Q(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}y})\n$$\n\nNow, since $P(x)$ does not depend on $y$ and $Q(y)$ does not depend on $x$, we can move the quantifiers to the front, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists y \\, Q(y) \\equiv \\exists x \\, \\exists y \\, (P(x) \\land Q(y))\n$$\n\nThe final expression reads\n\n$$\n\\exists x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: \\big( 4 \\mid x \\:\\land\\: { {y} \\textrm{ is prime}} \\big).\n$$\n\nSo, we get our prenex normal form, but we now have two variables instead of one. Finally, notice that the prenex form is not unique since we could change other occurrences of $x$, or use $t$ instead of $y$ for the new variable, etc. As a result, we can obtain slightly different resulting expressions." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186199573 1064 "Let's find a prenex normal form of the following expression:\n\n$$\nx\n$$\n\nDenoting\n\n- $P(x){:} \\: 4 \\mid x$,\n- $x$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe can't \"factor out\" the quantifier since, in general,\n\n$$\n\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x) \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\not\\equiv}\\exists x \\, (P(x) \\land Q(x))\n$$\n\nHowever, there is a workaround. If a variable is bound, we can't substitute anything into it. So, we can change any such variable