zes $n_1$ and $n_2$ be drawn from these populations, giving sample means $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$, and the pooled sample variance $s_p^2$.\n\nThen, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1 - \\mu_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) - t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) + t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}} \\: \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with\n\n$$\n\\nu = n_x +n_y -2\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nThe confidence limits (i.e., endpoints of our interval) are given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}\n$$\n\nAs usual, each part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is an estimate of the difference between the population means,\n- $S_p\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}$ is the standard error,\n- $t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score, and\n- $E = t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}$ is the margin of error.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\underbrace{\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}_{\\text{estimate}}\\\\[0.5em]\\pm\\;\\overbrace{\\underbrace{t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}}_{t\\text{-score}}\\cdot\\underbrace{\\color{red}S_p\\cdot\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x}+\\dfrac{1}{n_y}}}_{\\text{standard error}}}^{\\text{margin of error}}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left({\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}-[\\text{margin of error}],\\quad {\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}+[\\text{margin of error}]\\right) \\\\\n&\\left({\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}-[t\\text{-score}]\\cdot[{\\color{red}\\text{standard error}}],\\quad {\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}+[t\\text{-score}]\\cdot[{\\color{red}\\text{standard error}}]\\right) \\\\\n&\\left({\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}-t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}\\cdot{\\color{red}S_p\\cdot\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x}+\\dfrac{1}{n_y}}},\\quad {\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}+t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}\\cdot{\\color{red}S_p\\cdot\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x}+\\dfrac{1}{n_y}}}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note that if the underlying populations $X$ and $Y$ are not normally distributed, we can still use the above procedure to construct a confidence interval *provided that* the sample sizes $n_x$ and $n_y$ are sufficiently large (typically,\n\n$$\nn_x, n_y \\geq 30\n$$\n\n)." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186205093 1064 "Suppose we have two normal populations with unknown population means $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ and the same unknown population variance $\\sigma^2$. Let two independent random samples of sizes $n_1$ and $n_2$ be drawn from these populations, giving sample means $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$, and the pooled sample variance $s_p^2$.\n\nThen, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1 - \\mu_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) - t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) + t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}} \\: \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with\n\n$$\n\\nu = n_x +n_y -2\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nThe confidence limits (i.e., endpoints of our interval) are given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}\n$$\n\nAs usual, each part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is an estimate of the difference between the population means,\n- $S_p\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}$ is the standard error,\n- $t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score, and\n- $E = t_{\\nu,\\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}$ is the margin of error.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\nt\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n((x^(―) - y^(―)) - [margin of error],\\begin{vmatrix}(x^(―) - y^(―)) + [margin of error]) \\\\ ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - [[math]t[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error], \\mid (x^(―) - y^(―)) + [[math]t[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error]) \\\\ ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - t_{ν, α/2} \\cdot S_{p} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n_{x}} + \\frac{1}{n_{y}}},\\end{vmatrix}(x^(―) - y^(―)) + t_{ν, α/2} \\cdot S_{p} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n_{x}} + \\frac{1}{n_{y}}})\n$$\n\nFinally, note that if the underlying populations $X$ and $Y$ are not normally distributed, we can still use the above procedure to construct a confidence interval *provided that* the sample sizes $n_x$ and $n_y$ are sufficiently large (typically,\n\n$$\nn_x, n_y \\geq 30\n$$\n\n)." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186205139 1064 "Suppose we have two random samples of size $n_1$ and $n_2$ from two sufficiently large populations $p_1$ and $p_2$ with sample proportions $\\widehat{\\,p}_1$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}_2$, respectively. Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n_1 \\widehat{\\,p}_1 > 5, \\qquad n_1 (1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1) > 5$,\n- $n_2 \\widehat{\\,p}_2 > 5, \\qquad n_2 (1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2) > 5$.\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence limits for the population difference of proportions $p_1-p_2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2 \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, and\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score.\n\nThe quantity\n\n$$\nE =z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nis called the **margin of error.**\n\nSo, we have\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\widehat{p}_1-\\widehat{p}_2}_{\\text{estimate}}\\\\[0.5em]\\pm\\;\\overbrace{\\underbrace{z_{\\alpha/2}}_{z\\text{-score}}\\cdot\\underbrace{\\color{red}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{p}_1(1-\\widehat{p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{p}_2(1-\\widehat{p}_2)}{n_2}}}_{\\text{standard error}}}^{\\text{margin of error}}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n\n\nand our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\widehat{p}_1-\\widehat{p}_2}-[\\text{margin of error}],\\quad {\\color{blue}\\widehat{p}_1-\\widehat{p}_2}+[\\text{margin of error}]\\right)\n$$\n\nThroughout this lesson, you may assume that if a population is \"large\", it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it. We'll look at cases with small samples in future lessons." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186205139 1064 "Suppose we have two random samples of size $n_1$ and $n_2$ from two sufficiently large populations $p_1$ and $p_2$ with sample proportions $\\widehat{\\,p}_1$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}_2$, respectively. Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n_1 \\widehat{\\,p}_1 > 5, \\qquad n_1 (1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1) > 5$,\n- $n_2 \\widehat{\\,p}_2 > 5, \\qquad n_2 (1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2) > 5$.\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence limits for the population difference of proportions $p_1-p_2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2 \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, and\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score.\n\nThe quantity\n\n$$\nE =z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nis called the **margin of error.**\n\nSo, we have\n$z$\n\nand our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(p^(^)_{1} - p^(^)_{2} - [margin of error], \\mid p^(^)_{1} - p^(^)_{2} + [margin of error])\n$$\n\nThroughout this lesson, you may assume that if a population is \"large\", it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it. We'll look at cases with small samples in future lessons." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186205141 1064 "Let's consider the following first-order recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1} + 2^n, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nThis relation is *inhomogeneous* because the right-hand side contains\n\n$$\nf(n) = 2^n\n$$\n\nwhich is different from zero.\n\nWe say that the recurrence relation is subject to **exponential forcing** because $f(n)$ is an exponential function in this case.\n\nRecall that to find the general solution to a linear inhomogeneous recurrence relation, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous equation.\n- **Step 2**: Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n- **Step 3**: Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary and particular solutions:\n$a_n = a_n^{(h)} + a_n^{(p)}$\n\nTo find the general solution of the given recurrence relation, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous equation.\n\nThe associated homogeneous recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nand this has the general solution\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot 4^n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nSince the inhomogeneous function $g(n)=2^n$ is an exponential with base $2$ (which is not the base of the exponential in the complementary solution), we assume the particular solution is of the same form, i.e.\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 2^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n - 1}^{(p)} &= α \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original relation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n}^{(p)} &= 4a_{n - 1}^{(p)} + 2^{n} \\\\\nα \\cdot 2^{n} &= 4α \\cdot 2^{n - 1} + 2^{n} \\\\\nα \\cdot 2^{n} &= 2α \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} + 2^{n} \\\\\nα \\cdot 2^{n} &= 2α \\cdot 2^{n} + 2^{n} \\\\\nα \\cdot 2^{n} &= 2α \\cdot 2^{n} + 1 \\cdot 2^{n} \\\\\nα &= 2α + 1 \\\\\nα &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} =-2^n\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the general solution.\n\nTo find the general solution, we simply sum $a_n^{(h)}$ and $a_n^{(p)}$. Therefore, our general solution is\n\n$$\na_n = A \\cdot 4^n - 2^n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFinally, note that if we're also given the first term of the recurrence relation, we can use this information to find the constant $A$. We'll see an example of this later in the lesson." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186205141 1064 "Let's consider the following first-order recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1} + 2^n, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nThis relation is *inhomogeneous* because the right-hand side contains\n\n$$\nf(n) = 2^n\n$$\n\nwhich is different from zero.\n\nWe say that the recurrence relation is subject to **exponential forcing** because $f(n)$ is an exponential function in this case.\n\nRecall that to find the general solution to a linear inhomogeneous recurrence relation, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous equation.\n- **Step 2**: Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n- **Step 3**: Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary and particular solutions:\n$a_n = a_n^{(h)} + a_n^{(p)}$\n\nTo find the general solution of the given recurrence relation, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous equation.\n\nThe associated homogeneous recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nand this has the general solution\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot 4^n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nSince the inhomogeneous function $g(n)=2^n$ is an exponential with base $2$ (which is not the base of the exponential in the complementary solution), we assume the particular solution is of the same form, i.e.\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 2^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n - 1}^{(p)} &= α \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original relation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {a}_{n}^{( p )} & \\displaystyle  = 4 {a}_{n - 1}^{( p )} + {2}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {2}^{n} & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\alpha  \\cdot  {2}^{n - 1} + {2}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {2}^{n} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\alpha  \\cdot  2 \\cdot  {2}^{n - 1} + {2}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {2}^{n} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\alpha  \\cdot  {2}^{n} + {2}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  \\cancel{ {2}^{n}} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\alpha  \\cdot  \\cancel{ {2}^{n}} + 1 \\cdot  \\cancel{ {2}^{n}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\alpha  + 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  & \\displaystyle  = - 1\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} =-2^n\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the general solution.\n\nTo find the general solution, we simply sum $a_n^{(h)}$ and $a_n^{(p)}$. Therefore, our general solution is\n\n$$\na_n = A \\cdot 4^n - 2^n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFinally, note that if we're also given the first term of the recurrence relation, we can use this information to find the constant $A$. We'll see an example of this later in the lesson." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186205142 1064 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1} + 5\\cdot 4^n, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nLet's apply our three-step process to find the general solution of this recurrence relation.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous relation.\n\nThe associated homogeneous recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = 4a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 2\n$$\n\nand this has the general solution\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot 4^n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe now have a slight problem. The forcing function of the inhomogeneous equation\n\n$$\nf(n) = 5\\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nis a constant multiple of the complementary solution $a_n^{(h)}$. So, we *cannot* choose the particular solution\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nbecause solutions of this type are already incorporated into the complementary solution. We say that $f(n)$ and $a_n^{(h)}$ are **linearly dependent.**\n\nIn cases such as this, we need to choose the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha n \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\na_{n-1}^{(p)} = \\alpha (n-1) \\cdot 4^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original relation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n}^{(p)} &= 4a_{n - 1}^{(p)} + 5 \\cdot 4^{n}, \\\\\nαn \\cdot 4^{n} &= 4α(n - 1) \\cdot 4^{n - 1} + 5 \\cdot 4^{n}, \\\\\nαn \\cdot 4^{n} &= α(n - 1) \\cdot 4^{n} + 5 \\cdot 4^{n}, \\\\\nαn \\cdot 4^{n} &= α(n - 1) \\cdot 4^{n} + 5 \\cdot 4^{n}, \\\\\nαn &= α(n - 1) + 5, \\\\\nαn &= αn - α + 5, \\\\\n0 &= - α + 5, \\\\\nα &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular