 \\: y$ if and only if $x \\gt y$.\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\:: \\: x \\gt y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAccording to the definition, to express the inverse $R^{-1}$, we need to swap the variables $x$ and $y$ in the condition:\n\n$$\nx\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nR^{-1} = {(x, y) ∈ Z^{2}: y > x}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186205267 1064 "For a relation $R$ defined over $A$ and $B$, the **inverse relation** $R^{-1}$ is the relation defined over $B$ and $A$ that contains all the pairs from $R$ where the first and second components are swapped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{-1} &= {(y, x) ∈ B \\cdot A: xRy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are simply labels, we often rewrite $x\\to y$ and $y\\to x$ writing out our definition of $R^{-1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{-1} &= {(x, y) ∈ B \\cdot A: yRx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither of the two definitions above is valid.\n\nFor example, consider the relation $R$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\gt y$.\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\:: \\: x \\gt y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAccording to the definition, to express the inverse $R^{-1}$, we need to swap the variables $x$ and $y$ in the condition:\n\n$$\nx \\gt y \n\\qquad\n\\overset{ {\\textrm{swap } {x} \\textrm{ and } {y}}}{\\longrightarrow}\n\\qquad\ny \\gt x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nR^{-1} = {(x, y) ∈ Z^{2}: y > x}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186205280 1064 "Proposition.\n\n> *Suppose $n$ is an integer. Then, $15 \\mid n$ if and only if $3 \\mid n$ and $5 \\mid n$.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *We need to show the following:*\n> $$\n{ {15 ∣ n}} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big( { {3 ∣ n}} \\quad \\land \\quad { {5 ∣ n}} \\big)\n$$\n> *This is the same as proving two implications.*\n> ***Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$*** *We need to show that*\n> $$\n{ {15 ∣ n}} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big( { {3 ∣ n}} \\quad \\land \\quad { {5 ∣ n}} \\big).\n$$\n> *If $15 \\mid n$, then