nTherefore, the **negative** solution to the equation is\n\n$$\nn = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-12}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186208317 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $20$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 20 {n}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\cdot  144 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 {n}^{2}}{20}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 \\cdot  144}{20}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{20} {n}^{2}}{\\cancel{20}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{20} \\cdot  144}{\\cancel{20}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {n}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 144\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} &= 144 \\\\\n\\sqrt{n^{2}} &= \\sqrt{144} \\\\\n\\mid n \\mid &= 12 \\\\\nn &= ± 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **negative** solution to the equation is\n\n$$\nn = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-12}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186208346 1074 "The cosine of an acute angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle is given by\n$\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{ {\\text{adjacent to } {\\theta }}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}.$\n\nA sketch of such a triangle $\\triangle LMN$ is given below where angle $\\angle L$ is a right angle,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle N) = \\dfrac{28}{53}\n$$\n\nand $k > 0$.\n\n![](images/16/16389b762708dfc8ba5ac9656f2be5a73ebf42f7a51bea5dfa13a40a77d4d678.png)\n\n(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)\n\nBy the Pythagorean theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM^{2} &= MN^{2} - LN^{2} \\\\\n&= (53k)^{2} - (28k)^{2} \\\\\n&= 2809k^{2} - 784k^{2} \\\\\n&= 2025k^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$LM = 45k$.\n\nNow, for angle $\\angle M$, the adjacent side is $LM$ and the hypotenuse is $MN$.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle M) = \\dfrac{LM}{MN} = \\dfrac{45k}{53k} = \\dfrac{45}{53}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle M) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{45}{53}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186208346 1074 "The cosine of an acute angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle is given by\n$θ$\n\nA sketch of such a triangle $\\triangle LMN$ is given below where angle $\\angle L$ is a right angle,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle N) = \\dfrac{28}{53}\n$$\n\nand $k > 0$.\n\n![](images/16/16389b762708dfc8ba5ac9656f2be5a73ebf42f7a51bea5dfa13a40a77d4d678.png)\n\n(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)\n\nBy the Pythagorean theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM^{2} &= MN^{2} - LN^{2} \\\\\n&= (53k)^{2} - (28k)^{2} \\\\\n&= 2809k^{2} - 784k^{2} \\\\\n&= 2025k^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$LM = 45k$.\n\nNow, for angle $\\angle M$, the adjacent side is $LM$ and the hypotenuse is $MN$.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle M) = \\dfrac{LM}{MN} = \\dfrac{45k}{53k} = \\dfrac{45}{53}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\cos(m\\angle M) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{45}{53}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186208355 1074 "First, let's sketch a figure (not drawn to scale).\n\n![](images/6f/6f0520243bbd65d3708e086c56c2f387285f4a8d21dc3547f899f13fc0501049.png)\n\nSince triangle $JKL$ is similar to triangle $OPQ$, and $J$ corresponds to $O$, we know that $\\angle J \\cong \\angle O$. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin O = \\sin J\n$$\n\nWe are given\n\n$$\n\\tan J = \\dfrac{33}{56}\n$$\n\nThe tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side:\n\n$$\n\\tan J = \\dfrac{KL}{JL} = \\dfrac{33}{56}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the legs of triangle $JKL$ are in a $33:56$ ratio. So we let $KL = 33k$ and $JL = 56k$ for some positive constant $k$. We use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the hypotenuse $JK{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJK &= \\sqrt{(33k)^{2} + (56k)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1089k^{2} + 3136k^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4225k^{2}} \\\\\n&= 65k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\sin J$, which is the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin J &= \\frac{KL}{JK} \\\\\n&= \\frac{33k}{65k} \\\\\n&= 33 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186208355 1074 "First, let's sketch a figure (not drawn to scale).\n\n![](images/6f/6f0520243bbd65d3708e086c56c2f387285f4a8d21dc3547f899f13fc0501049.png)\n\nSince triangle $JKL$ is similar to triangle $OPQ$, and $J$ corresponds to $O$, we know that $\\angle J \\cong \\angle O$. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin O = \\sin J\n$$\n\nWe are given\n\n$$\n\\tan J = \\dfrac{33}{56}\n$$\n\nThe tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side:\n\n$$\n\\tan J = \\dfrac{KL}{JL} = \\dfrac{33}{56}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the legs of triangle $JKL$ are in a $33:56$ ratio. So we let $KL = 33k$ and $JL = 56k$ for some positive constant $k$. We use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the hypotenuse $JK{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJK &= \\sqrt{(33k)^{2} + (56k)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1089k^{2} + 3136k^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4225k^{2}} \\\\\n&= 65k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\sin J$, which is the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\sin   J & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{K L}{J K}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{33 k}{65 k}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{33}{65}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186208357 1074 "First, let's sketch a figure (not drawn to scale).\n\n![](images/38/38531a3a5b1657d4ffa6c4af35b484428d958bb6ff484b7acbc081ec35fbea2f.png)\n\nWe are asked to find $\\tan H$ in triangle $HIJ$. To do this, we will use the definition of tangent along with corresponding side ratios.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\tan F = \\dfrac{12}{35}\n$$\n\nIn a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side, then we have\n\n$$\n\\tan F = \\dfrac{EG}{GF} = \\dfrac{12}{35}\n$$\n\nSince tria