 to $62%$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186209501 1071 "A health department surveyed $1,000$ residents who were selected at random from a small city and asked, “Have you received a flu vaccine in the past year?” Of those surveyed, $40 \\mathrm{\\% }$ responded “yes”.\n\nBased on this result, which of the following conclusions are appropriate?\n\n1. Of all residents in the city, approximately $40 \\mathrm{\\% }$ have received a flu vaccine in the past year.\n2. If another $1,000$ residents selected at random from the city were surveyed, the proportion who say “yes” is likely to be close to $40 \\mathrm{\\% } .$\n3. If $1,000$ residents selected at random from a different city were surveyed, the proportion who say “yes” is likely to be close to $40 \\mathrm{\\% } .$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186209501 1071 "A health department surveyed $1,000$ residents who were selected at random from a small city and asked, “Have you received a flu vaccine in the past year?” Of those surveyed, $40%$ responded “yes”.\n\nBased on this result, which of the following conclusions are appropriate?\n\n1. Of all residents in the city, approximately $40%$ have received a flu vaccine in the past year.\n2. If another $1,000$ residents selected at random from the city were surveyed, the proportion who say “yes” is likely to be close to $40%$.\n3. If $1,000$ residents selected at random from a different city were surveyed, the proportion who say “yes” is likely to be close to $40%$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186209961 1064 "In machine learning and linear algebra, it’s often convenient to express the Hadamard product of two vectors using matrix multiplication. If $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\odot \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v}) \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{u}) \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix product\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe notice that the left matrix is diagonal. So, we can write it as a Hadamard product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= diag(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's see a concrete example of expressing a Hadamard product as a matrix product in the next slide." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186209961 1064 "In machine learning and linear algebra, it’s often convenient to express the Hadamard product of two vectors using matrix multiplication. If $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\odot \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v}) \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{u}) \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix product\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nwe notice that the left matrix is diagonal. So, we can write it as a Hadamard product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= diag(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's see a concrete example of expressing a Hadamard product as a matrix product in the next slide." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186209962 1064 "Before we continue, note that the function $\\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v})$ is a **diagonal matrix** with the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb R^n$ on the diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}v_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & v_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & v_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at some concrete examples involving the Hadamard product." 13194139536831 1759