t model the situation. Let $x$ denote the volume of base solvent and $y$ denote the volume of disinfectant concentrate the team purchases.\n\n- The total cost of the solution is $0.90x+6.30y$. Since the team wants to spend less than $\\[math]414$, we have\n$0.90x+6.30y < 414$.\n- The total volume of the solution is $x+y$. Since the team needs to mix more than $170$ liters to maintain the shipping rate, we have\n$x+y > 170$.\n\nAlso, notice that\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nas the volumes must be non-negative.\nSo, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}0.90 x + 6.30 y < 414 \\\\ x + y > 170 \\\\ x \\ge  0 \\\\ y \\ge  0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's now plot the corresponding regions in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/23/23a62cc94d24c625d809a3c70faa3137775b317363529a870156af836b9be5ec.png)\n\nNotice that the corner point on the top of the feasible region has the coordinates approximately $(121.667,48.333)$, rounded to three decimal places. So, rounded to one decimal place, the maximum volume of disinfectant concentrate that potentially could satisfy the given constraints is $y=48.3$.\n\nNow, if $y=48.3$, then\n\n$$\nx \\mid > 170 - y; = 170 - 48.3; = 121.7\n$$\n\nSince the inequality is strict we take $x=121.8$.\n\nSubstituting $x=121.8$ and $y=48.3$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.90x + 6.30y &= 0.90(121.8) + 6.30(48.3) \\\\\n&= 413.91 \\\\\n&< 414✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, all the constraints are indeed satisfied. Therefore, the team can include a maximum of $48.3$ liters of disinfectant concentrate." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186212989 1074 "Notice that $(p-4)$ is a common factor on both sides of the equation.\n\nWe must isolate $(p-4)$ on one side of the equation. We start by adding $4(p-4)$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 6(p - 4) &= 2 - 4(p - 4) \\\\\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= 2 - 4(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) \\\\\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $14$ from both sides, and factor $(p-4)$ on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) - 14 &= 2 - 14 \\\\\n- 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-6 + 4)(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n- 2(p - 4) &= -12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $-2$ and isolate $(p - 4){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-2(p - 4))/(-2) &= \\frac{-12}{-2} \\\\\np - 4 &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained linear equation for $p$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np - 4 &= 6 \\\\\np - 4 + 4 &= 6 + 4 \\\\\np &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\np=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{10}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186212989 1074 "Notice that $(p-4)$ is a common factor on both sides of the equation.\n\nWe must isolate $(p-4)$ on one side of the equation. We start by adding $4(p-4)$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 14 - 6 ( p - 4 ) & \\displaystyle  = 2 - 4 ( p - 4 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 14 - 6 ( p - 4 ) + 4 ( p - 4 ) & \\displaystyle  = 2 - \\cancel{4 ( p - 4 )} + \\cancel{4 ( p - 4 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle 14 - 6 ( p - 4 ) + 4 ( p - 4 ) & \\displaystyle  = 2\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $14$ from both sides, and factor $(p-4)$ on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) - 14 &= 2 - 14 \\\\\n- 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-6 + 4)(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n- 2(p - 4) &= -12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $-2$ and isolate $(p - 4){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-2(p - 4))/(-2) &= \\frac{-12}{-2} \\\\\np - 4 &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained linear equation for $p$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np - 4 &= 6 \\\\\np - 4 + 4 &= 6 + 4 \\\\\np &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\np=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{10}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186213385 1071 "A fully ch