92186045436 false] [17592186214217 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to simplify fractions of fractions with algebraic expressions. The core idea is much like simplifying numerical fractions.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div (15x + 60)\n$$\n\nNow, we write the divisor as a fraction over $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div \\dfrac{15x + 60}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nNotice that the denominator of the second fraction can be written as\n\n$$\n15x + 60 = 15(x + 4)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we multiply the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{15x + 60} &= \\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot (1)/(15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30 \\cdot 1)/((x + 4) \\cdot 15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30)/(15(x + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify by canceling out common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(30)/(15(x + 4)^{2}) &= (2 \\cdot 15)/(15(x + 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2)/((x + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60} = \\dfrac{2}{(x + 4)^2}\n$$\n\nvalid for all\n\n$$\nx\\neq -4\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214217 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to simplify fractions of fractions with algebraic expressions. The core idea is much like simplifying numerical fractions.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div (15x + 60)\n$$\n\nNow, we write the divisor as a fraction over $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div \\dfrac{15x + 60}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nNotice that the denominator of the second fraction can be written as\n\n$$\n15x + 60 = 15(x + 4)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we multiply the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{15x + 60} &= \\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot (1)/(15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30 \\cdot 1)/((x + 4) \\cdot 15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30)/(15(x + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify by canceling out common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{30}{15 ( x + 4 {)}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\cdot  \\cancel{15}}{\\cancel{15} ( x + 4 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{( x + 4 {)}^{2}}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60} = \\dfrac{2}{(x + 4)^2}\n$$\n\nvalid for all\n\n$$\nx\\neq -4\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214249 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(1, 000\\frac{m}{km})^{2}|= 1, 000^{2}\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}; = 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.9\\frac{km^{2}}{h} &= 0.9\\frac{km^{2}}{h} \\cdot 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3, 600}\\frac{h}{s} \\\\\n&= (0.9 \\cdot 1, 000, 000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3, 600})\\frac{km^{2}}{h} \\cdot \\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{h}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{900, 000}{3, 600}\\frac{m^{2}}{s} \\\\\n&= 250\\frac{m^{2}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214249 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(1, 000\\frac{m}{km})^{2}|= 1, 000^{2}\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}; = 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214256 1064 "When we have data from a random sample, we can use this data to *estimate* (or **infer**) characteristics of the population from which the sample was drawn.\n\nFor example, suppose there are $500$ employees at a company. A random sample of employees was conducted, and each participant was asked if they planned to attend the upcoming industry conference. Of those surveyed, $62\\%$ responded that they planned to attend.\n\nFrom this sample, we can *estimate* how many employees in the *entire company* plan to attend by applying the sample percentage ($62\\%$) to the total population ($500$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n500 \\cdot 62% &= 500 \\cdot 0.62 \\\\\n&= 310\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our **best estimate** is that $310$ employees plan to attend the conference.\n\nIt's important to note that this is an *estimate* of the total number of employees who plan to attend the conference, and the exact number *may differ from this!* We'll discuss this at more length in a future lesson.\n\nFinally, please note that, throughout this lesson, all samples will be carried out by *randomly selecting* units from the population to reduce sample bias." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214256 1064 "When we have data from a random sample, we can use this data to *estimate* (or **infer**) characteristics of the population from which the sample was drawn.\n\nFor example, suppose there are $500$ employees at a company. A random sample of employees was conducted, and each participant was asked if they planned to attend the upcoming industry conference. Of those surveyed, $62\\%$ responded that they planned to attend.\n\nFrom this sample, we can *estimate* how many employees in the *entire company* plan to attend by applying the sample percentage ($62\\%$) to the total population ($500$):\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 500 \\cdot  62 \\mathrm{\\% } & \\displaystyle  = 500 \\cdot  0.62 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 310\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our **best estimate** is that $310$ employees plan to attend the conference.\n\nIt's important to note that this is an *estimate* of the total number of employees who plan to attend the conference, and the exact number *may differ from this!* We'll discuss this at more length in a future lesson.\n\nFinally, please note that, throughout this lesson, all samples will be carried out by *randomly selecting* units from the population to reduce sample bias." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214257 1064 "When we use data from a random sample to make predictions about an entire population, our results are *estimates*, not exact values. Even if the sample is chosen carefully, there is always some uncertainty about how the results generalize to the entire population, because we are not measuring every individual in the population.\n\nFor this reason, we often speak in terms of a **best estimate**. The best estimate uses the information from the sample to predict what is most likely true for the population, while recognizing that the actual population value may differ.\n\nFor example, suppose $2,500$ people visited a flea market. The staff randomly asked $125$ visitors whether they bought jewelry, and found that $25$ of them did.\nWhat is the most appropriate conclusion we can draw from this result?\n\nFrom the random sample, the proportion who bought jewelry is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{125} = 0.20 = 20\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $20\\%$ of those sampled bought jewelry.\n\nTo estimate how many of the $2,500$ visitors bought jewelry, we scale by this proportion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2, 500 \\cdot 20% &= 2, 500 \\cdot 0.20 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause this estimate is based on a sample, we cannot say that *exactly* $500$ people bought jewelry. Instead, the most appropriate conclusion is:\n\n> *\"The best estimate for the number of visitors who bought jewelry is $500$.\"*\n\nThis example highlights the key idea: sample-based predictions are *approximate*, not certain. We use phrases like *best estimate* to emphasize this vital distinction." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214257 1064 "When we use data from a random sample to make predictions about an entire population, our results are *estimates*, not exact values. Even if the sample is chosen carefully, there is always some uncertainty about how the results generalize to the entire population, because we are not measuring every individual in the population.\n\nFor this reason, we often speak in terms of a **best estimate**. The best estimate uses the information from the sample to predict what is most likely true for the population, while recognizing that the actual population value may differ.\n\nFor example, suppose $2,500$ people visited a flea market. The staff randomly asked $125$ visitors whether they bought jewelry, and found that $25$ of them did.\nWhat is the most appropriate conclusion we can draw from this result?\n\nFrom the random sample, the proportion who bought jewelry is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{125} = 0.20 = 20\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $20\\%$ of those sampled bought jewelry.\n\nTo estimate how many of the $2,500$ visitors bought jewelry, we scale by this proportion:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 2 , 500 \\cdot  20 \\mathrm{\\% } & \\displaystyle  = 2 , 500 \\cdot  0.20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 500.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nBecause this estimate is based on a sample, we cannot say that *exactly* $500$ people bought jewelry. Instead, the most appropriate conclusion is:\n\n> *\"The best estimate for the number of visitors who bought jewelry is $500$.\"*\n\nThis example highlights the key idea: sample-based predictions are *approximate*, not certain. We use phrases like *best estimate* to emphasize this vital distinction." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214262 1064 "Systems of equations sometimes contain unknown constants (called **parameters**) that affect the number of solutions to the system. In such cases, two questions we may wish to ask are:\n\n- For what values of the parameter does the system have infinitely many solutions?\n- For what values of the parameter does the system have no solutions?\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to learn how to answer these questions.\n\nConsider the system of equations below, where $d$ is an unknown constant.\n\n$$\n{7x - 2y = 11; 21x - 6y = d\n$$\n\nSuppose we're told that this system has *infinitely many* solutions. Given this information, what is the value of $d$?\n\nRecall that a system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the *same line.* Moreover, two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional. This gives us the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{21} = \\left(\\dfrac{-2}{-6}\\right) = \\dfrac{11}{d}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{21} = \\left(\\dfrac{-2}{-6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of $d$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{11}{d}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation for $d$ by cross-multiplying, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{11}{d} \\\\\n1 \\cdot d &= 3 \\cdot 11 \\\\\nd &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $d=33$, the two equations describe the same line, and the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nA sketch of the situation is given below. Note that the two lines coincide, and the equations of the lines are indeed multiples of each other.\n\n![](images/59/590b44faaea9e545880619d02edeaa83867b2afb95bb0db82778c294ce7d02f1.png)\n\nWe can use the same idea to solve for unknown parameters given a system containing fractional coefficients. Let's see an example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214262 1064 "Systems of equations sometimes contain unknown constants (called **parameters**) that affect the number of solutions to the system. In such cases, two questions we may wish to ask are:\n\n- For what values of the parameter does the system have infinitely many solutions?\n- For what values of the parameter does the system have no solutions?\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to learn how to answer these questions.\n\nConsider the system of equations below, where $d$ is an unknown constant.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 x - 2 y = 11 \\\\ 21 x - 6 y = d\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSuppose we're told that this system has *infinitely many* solutions. Given this information, what is the value of $d$?\n\nRecall that a system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the *same line.* Moreover, two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional. This gives us the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{21} = \\left(\\dfrac{-2}{-6}\\right) = \\dfrac{11}{d}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{21} = \\left(\\dfrac{-2}{-6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of $d$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{11}{d}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation for $d$ by cross-multiplying, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{11}{d} \\\\\n1 \\cdot d &= 3 \\cdot 11 \\\\\nd &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $d=33$, the two equations describe the same line, and the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nA sketch of the situation is given below. Note that the two lines coincide, and the equations of the lines are indeed multiples of each other.\n\n![](images/59/590b44faaea9e545880619d02edeaa83867b2afb95bb0db82778c294ce7d02f1.png)\n\nWe can use the same idea to solve for unknown parameters given a system containing fractional coefficients. Let's see an example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214268 1064 "Let's now see how to determine an unknown parameter when a system of linear equations has no solutions.\n\nConsider the system of equations below, where $t$ is a constant.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{8}} x - 2 \\\\ y = 14 t x + 9\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $t$?\n\nRecall that a system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent *distinct parallel lines.* This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nIn our case, both equations are in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- The first equation, $y = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{8}}x \\:{\\color{red}-\\: 2}$, has a slope of $\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{8}$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{red}-2}$.\n- The second equation, $y={\\color{blue}14t}x + {\\color{red}9}$, has a slope of $\\color{blue}14t$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{red}9}$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts ($\\color{red}-2$ and $\\color{red}9$), the slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, equating the slopes and solving for $t$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{8}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\color{blue}14 t} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{8 \\cdot  14}} & \\displaystyle  = t \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{8 \\cdot  2 \\cdot  7}} & \\displaystyle  = t \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{7}}{8 \\cdot  2 \\cdot  \\cancel{7}}} & \\displaystyle  = t \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8 \\cdot  2}} & \\displaystyle  = t \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{16}} & \\displaystyle  = t \\\\ \\displaystyle t & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{16}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac1{16}\n$$\n\nthe two equations describe distinct parallel lines, and the system has no solutions.\n\nA sketch of the situation is given below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1c0b802d491a8a0ac8bbc2c7091a2cbebcf8208910d8e5d7305b68ee44f3b06.png)" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214268 1064 "Let's now see how to determine an unknown parameter when a system of linear equations has no solutions.\n\nConsider the system of equations below, where $t$ is a constant.\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{7}{8}x - 2; y = 14tx + 9\n$$\n\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $t$?\n\nRecall that a system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent *distinct parallel lines.* This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nIn our case, both equations are in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- The first equation, $y = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{8}}x \\:{\\color{red}-\\: 2}$, has a slope of $\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{8}$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{red}-2}$.\n- The second equation, $y={\\color{blue}14t}x + {\\color{red}9}$, has a slope of $\\color{blue}14t$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{red}9}$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts ($\\color{red}-2$ and $\\color{red}9$), the slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, equating the slopes and solving for $t$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8} &= 14t \\\\\n\\frac{7}{8 \\cdot 14} &= t \\\\\n\\frac{7}{8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7} &= t \\\\\n\\frac{7}{8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7} &= t \\\\\n\\frac{1}{8 \\cdot 2} &= t \\\\\n\\frac{1}{16} &= t \\\\\nt &= \\frac{1}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac1{16}\n$$\n\nthe two equations describe distinct parallel lines, and the system has no solutions.\n\nA sketch of the situation is given below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1c0b802d491a8a0ac8bbc2c7091a2cbebcf8208910d8e5d7305b68ee44f3b06.png)" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214276 1064 "Let's consider our system of equations once again.\n\n$$\n{7x + 10y = 80; 10x + 7y = 73\n$$\n\nRecall that we wish to calculate the value of $x+y$.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation $(7)$ equals the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation, and vice versa.\n\n$$\n{7x + 10y = 80; 10x + 7y = 73\n$$\n\nBecause of this symmetry, if we *add* the two equations, both variables will appear with the *same coefficient*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 10y &= 80 \\\\\n10x + 7y &= 73 \\\\\n(7x + 10x) + (10y + 7y) &= 80 + 73 \\\\\n17x + 17y &= 153\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation for $x + y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17x + 17y &= 153 \\\\\n17(x + y) &= 153 \\\\\nx + y &= \\frac{153}{17} \\\\\nx + y &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214276 1064 "Let's consider our system of equations once again.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 x + 10 y = 80 \\\\ 10 x + 7 y = 73\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nRecall that we wish to calculate the value of $x+y$.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation $(7)$ equals the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation, and vice versa.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\color{red}7} x + {\\color{blue}10} y = 80 \\\\ {\\color{blue}10} x + {\\color{red}7} y = 73\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nBecause of this symmetry, if we *add* the two equations, both variables will appear with the *same coefficient*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 10y &= 80 \\\\\n10x + 7y &= 73 \\\\\n(7x + 10x) + (10y + 7y) &= 80 + 73 \\\\\n17x + 17y &= 153\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation for $x + y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17x + 17y &= 153 \\\\\n17(x + y) &= 153 \\\\\nx + y &= \\frac{153}{17} \\\\\nx + y &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214277 1064 "Now, consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5}{6}y =-18; x + \\frac{5}{6}y = 7\n$$\n\nHow can we quickly find the value of $x$?\n\nNotice that the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}y\n$$\n\noccurs in both equations.\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5}{6}y =-18; x + \\frac{5}{6}y = 7\n$$\n\nThis means we don't need to solve for the variable $y$ in the first equation to find $x$. This saves us a lot of time.\n\nFrom the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{5}{6}y} = {\\color{red}{-18}}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this value into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + \\frac{5}{6}y &= 7 \\\\\nx + (-18) &= 7 \\\\\nx - 18 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 18 &= 7 \\\\\nx - 18 + 18 &= 7 + 18 \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $25$.\n\nNotice that this problem used the substitution method. But since a repeated expression appeared in both equations, taking advantage of this made the solution much easier.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214277 1064 "Now, consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y = - 18 \\\\ x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y = 7\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nHow can we quickly find the value of $x$?\n\nNotice that the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}y\n$$\n\noccurs in both equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y} = - 18 \\\\ x + {\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y} = 7\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis means we don't need to solve for the variable $y$ in the first equation to find $x$. This saves us a lot of time.\n\nFrom the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{5}{6}y} = {\\color{red}{-18}}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this value into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + \\frac{5}{6}y &= 7 \\\\\nx + (-18) &= 7 \\\\\nx - 18 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 18 &= 7 \\\\\nx - 18 + 18 &= 7 + 18 \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $25$.\n\nNotice that this problem used the substitution method. But since a repeated expression appeared in both equations, taking advantage of this made the solution much easier.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214279 1064 "In this topic, we’ll learn how to solve systems of *nonlinear* equations by exploiting their structure. This means spotting repeated expressions or factors that allow us to simplify the system.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the nonlinear system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{4x - 12 = \\sqrt{14}; (4x - 12)^{2} = y\n$$\n\nHow can we quickly determine the value of $y$?\n\nWhen solving nonlinear systems, it is often helpful to check whether both equations share a *common expression*. If they do, we can isolate that expression in one equation and substitute it into the other, greatly simplifying our work.\n\nHere, notice that both equations involve the expression $4x - 12$.\n\n$$\n{4x - 12 = \\sqrt{14}; (4x - 12)^{2} = y\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n4x - 12 = \\sqrt{14}\n$$\n\nThis tells us precisely what $4x-12$ equals, and so we can replace it with this value wherever it appears.\n\nThus, substituting $\\sqrt{14}$ in place of $4x-12$ in the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x - 12)^{2} &= y \\\\\n(\\sqrt{14})^{2} &= y \\\\\n14 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y = 14$.\n\nThe key steps of this approach are the following:\n\n1. Identify a common expression.\n2. Solve for the common expression in one equation.\n3. Substitute its value into the other equation.\n\nLet’s see some more examples." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214279 1064 "In this topic, we’ll learn how to solve systems of *nonlinear* equations by exploiting their structure. This means spotting repeated expressions or factors that allow us to simplify the system.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the nonlinear system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 12 = \\sqrt{14} \\\\ ( 4 x - 12 {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nHow can we quickly determine the value of $y$?\n\nWhen solving nonlinear systems, it is often helpful to check whether both equations share a *common expression*. If they do, we can isolate that expression in one equation and substitute it into the other, greatly simplifying our work.\n\nHere, notice that both equations involve the expression $4x - 12$.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\color{blue}4 x - 12} = \\sqrt{14} \\\\ ( {\\color{blue}4 x - 12} {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n4x - 12 = \\sqrt{14}\n$$\n\nThis tells us precisely what $4x-12$ equals, and so we can replace it with this value wherever it appears.\n\nThus, substituting $\\sqrt{14}$ in place of $4x-12$ in the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x - 12)^{2} &= y \\\\\n(\\sqrt{14})^{2} &= y \\\\\n14 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y = 14$.\n\nThe key steps of this approach are the following:\n\n1. Identify a common expression.\n2. Solve for the common expression in one equation.\n3. Substitute its value into the other equation.\n\nLet’s see some more examples." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214283 1064 "Sometimes, we may want to introduce new variables into our system to expedite the solution process.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{4(5x) + 3(6y) = 29; - 4(5x) + 3(6y) = 13\n$$\n\nNotice that the expressions $5x$ and $6y$ each appear multiple times. To simplify the system, we introduce two *new variables* $w$ and $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nw=5x \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad z=6y\n$$\n\nThen, our system of equations can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{4w + 3z = 29; - 4w + 3z = 13\n$$\n\nWe will solve the new system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that when we add the two equations, the $4w$ terms will cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4w + 3z &= 29 \\\\\n- 4w + 3z &= 13 \\\\\n(3z + 3z) &= (29 + 13) \\\\\n6z &= 42 \\\\\nz &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we plug in $z=7$ into the first equation to find $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4w + 3z &= 29 \\\\\n4w + 3(7) &= 29 \\\\\n4w + 21 &= 29 \\\\\n4w &= 8 \\\\\nw &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for the original variables $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n{w = 2; z = 7⟹{5x = 2; 6y = 7⟹{x = \\frac{2}{5}; y = \\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThis method is similar to substitution, but instead of reusing an expression directly, we create new variables to represent complex pieces.\n\nLet's see another, slightly more complicated example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214283 1064 "Sometimes, we may want to introduce new variables into our system to expedite the solution process.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 ( {\\color{blue}5 x} ) + 3 ( {\\color{red}6 y} ) = 29 \\\\ - 4 ( {\\color{blue}5 x} ) + 3 ( {\\color{red}6 y} ) = 13\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNotice that the expressions $5x$ and $6y$ each appear multiple times. To simplify the system, we introduce two *new variables* $w$ and $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nw=5x \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad z=6y\n$$\n\nThen, our system of equations can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 {\\color{blue}w} + 3 {\\color{red}z} = 29 \\\\ - 4 {\\color{blue}w} + 3 {\\color{red}z} = 13\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the new system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that when we add the two equations, the $4w$ terms will cancel:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\cancel{4 w} + 3 z & \\displaystyle  = 29 \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{- 4 w} + 3 z & \\displaystyle  = 13 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle ( 3 z + 3 z ) & \\displaystyle  = ( 29 + 13 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 6 z & \\displaystyle  = 42 \\\\ \\displaystyle z & \\displaystyle  = 7\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we plug in $z=7$ into the first equation to find $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4w + 3z &= 29 \\\\\n4w + 3(7) &= 29 \\\\\n4w + 21 &= 29 \\\\\n4w &= 8 \\\\\nw &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for the original variables $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}w} = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}z} = 7\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\Longrightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}5 x} = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}6 y} = 7\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} \\Longrightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle x = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} \\\\ \\displaystyle y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{6}}\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis method is similar to substitution, but instead of reusing an expression directly, we create new variables to represent complex pieces.\n\nLet's see another, slightly more complicated example." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214307 1064 "Sometimes, we can simplify nonlinear systems of equations by factoring the equations first. This can reveal a repeated structure that we can exploit to solve the system efficiently.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} - {y}^{2} = 15 \\\\ x + y = 5\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNotice that we can factor the expression on the left-hand side of the first equation as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\nx^2-y^2=(x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( {\\color{blue}x + y} ) ( x - y ) = 15 \\\\ {\\color{blue}x + y} = {\\color{red}5}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression ${\\color{blue}{x+y}}$ is common to both equations. Moreover, we know from the second equation that this equals ${\\color{red}{5}}$.\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x+y}}={\\color{red}{5}}\n$$\n\ninto the first equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + y)(x - y) &= 15 \\\\\n(5)(x - y) &= 15 \\\\\nx - y &= \\frac{15}{5} \\\\\nx - y &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the simpler, linear system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\color{blue}x + y} = {\\color{red}5} \\\\ {\\color{purple}x - y} = {\\color{purple}3}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nHere, adding the two equations gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + y) + (x - y) &= 5 + 3 \\\\\n2x &= 8 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4$ into\n\n$$\nx+y=5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n(4)+y=5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(4,1)\n$$\n\nWe see from this example that even for nonlinear systems, identifying algebraic structures such as a difference of squares can transform the problem into a simpler linear system that's easier to solve." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186214307 1064 "Sometimes, we can simplify nonlinear systems of equations by factoring the equations first. This can reveal a repeated structure that we can exploit to solve the system efficiently.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{x^{2} - y^{2} = 15; x + y = 5\n$$\n\nNotice that we can factor the expression on the left-hand side of the first equation as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\nx^2-y^2=(x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x + y)(x - y) = 15; x + y = 5\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression ${\\color{blue}{x+y}}$ is common to both equations. Moreover, we know from the second equation that this equals ${\\color{red}{5}}$.\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x+y}}={\\color{red}{5}}\n$$\n\ninto the first equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + y)(x - y) &= 15 \\\\\n(5)(x - y) &= 15 \\\\\nx - y &= \\frac{15}{5} \\\\\nx - y &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the simpler, linear system:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 5; x - y = 3\n$$\n\nHere, adding the two equations gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + y) + (x - y) &= 5 + 3 \\\\\n2x &= 8 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4$ into\n\n$$\nx+y=5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n(4)+y=5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(4,1)\n$$\n\nWe see from this example that even for nonlinear systems, identifying algebraic structures such as a difference of squares can transform the problem into a simpler linear system that's easier to solve." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214316 1064 "In this lesson, we'll explore some techniques for solving systems of equations efficiently.\n\nTo motivate our discussion, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{7x + 10y = 80; 10x + 7y = 73\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to determine the quantity $x+y$. What's the easiest way to do this?\n\nWhen solving systems of linear equations, two primary techniques are available: the substitution method and the elimination method.\n\n- To solve this using *substitution*, we might start by expressing the first equation in terms of the variable $y$. By doing this, we get\n$y =-\\dfrac{7}{10}x + 10$.\nNotice we've introduced a fraction, which makes the remaining steps more awkward.\n- To solve this using *elimination*, on the other hand, we might choose to multiply the first equation by $7$ (the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation), the second equation by $10$ (the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation), before subtracting them to eliminate $y{:}$\n${(7x + 10y = 80) \\cdot (7); (10x + 7y = 73) \\cdot (10)$\nThe issue now is that we've created a system containing very large numbers.\n\nIt turns out that we can exploit the system's symmetry to solve it more efficiently. We'll talk about this next." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214316 1064 "In this lesson, we'll explore some techniques for solving systems of equations efficiently.\n\nTo motivate our discussion, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 x + 10 y = 80 \\\\ 10 x + 7 