e case where the coefficients are arranged with *opposite signs*:\n\n$$\n{ax - by = m; bx - ay = n\n$$\n\nIn this case, adding the equations produces equal coefficients of $x$ and $y$, but with *opposite signs*, allowing us to solve quickly for $x-y$.\n\nLet's see a concrete example of the case with opposite signs." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214318 1064 "In algebra, equations often involve fractions (rational expressions) with more than one variable. The key strategy is to eliminate the denominator and then carefully isolate the variable we want to solve for.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the relation between three distinct positive numbers $p$, $q$, and $h{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{q}{7h}\n$$\n\nWe want to express $q$ in terms of $p$ and $h$.\n\nTo eliminate the denominator, we multiply *both sides* of the equation by $7h$ to remove the fractions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= -\\frac{q}{7h} \\\\\np \\cdot 7h &= -\\frac{q}{7h} \\cdot 7h \\\\\np \\cdot 7h &= -\\frac{q}{7h} \\cdot 7h \\\\\n7hp &= -q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both sides* by $-1$ to isolate $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7hp \\cdot (-1) &= -q \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n- 7hp &= q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is\n$q = -7hp$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186214318 1064 "In algebra, equations often involve fractions (rational expressions) with more than one variable. The key strategy is to eliminate the denominator and then carefully isolate the variable we want to solve for.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the relation between three distinct positive numbers $p$, $q$, and $h{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{q}{7h}\n$$\n\nWe want to express $q$ in terms of $p$ and $h$.\n\nTo eliminate the denominator, we multiply *both sides* of the equation by $7h$ to remove the fractions.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle p & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{q}{7 h}} \\\\ \\displaystyle p \\cdot  7 h & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\