unt is $\\[math]14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\[/math]21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\[math]21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n140% \\cdot p &= 21 \\\\\n\\frac{140}{100} \\cdot p &= 21 \\\\\n1.4p &= 21 \\\\\np &= \\frac{21}{1.4} \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\[/math]15$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186216965 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\[math]35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[/math]35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  35 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[math]14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\[/math]21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\[math]21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displayst