n what the event organizer paid per ticket. What was the event organizer's cost for each ticket?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186217744 1071 "A concert ticket has a regular price of $$42.00$. During a special promotion, the ticket is sold at a price that is $60%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40%$ more than what the event organizer paid per ticket. What was the event organizer's cost for each ticket?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186217745 1071 "A pharmacy buys electric toothbrushes at a set wholesale cost and sells them at a retail price that is $275 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining toothbrush is sold at a discounted price. The retail price is $120 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the discounted price. If the discounted price of a toothbrush is $53.90$, what is the discounted price of an electric toothbrush?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186217745 1071 "A pharmacy buys electric toothbrushes at a set wholesale cost and sells them at a retail price that is $275%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining toothbrush is sold at a discounted price. The retail price is $120%$ of the discounted price. If the discounted price of a toothbrush is $53.90$, what is the discounted price of an electric toothbrush?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186217746 1071 "A movie ticket has a set regular price. During a special promotion, the ticket is sold at a price that is $40 \\mathrm{\\% }$ less than the regular price. The promotional sale price is $130 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the theater's cost per ticket. If the theater's cost per ticket is $$3.60$, what was the regular price for each ticket?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186217746 1071 "A movie ticket has a set regular price. During a special promotion, the ticket is sold at a price that is $40%$ less than the regular price. The promotional sale price is $130%$ of the theater's cost per ticket. If the theater's cost per ticket is $$3.60$, what was the regular price for each ticket?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186217751 1071 "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 1 \\\\ 2 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ 3 x + 6 y = 3\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186217751 1071 "${x + 2y = 1; 2x + 4y = 2; 3x + 6y = 3$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186218217 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to translate constraints into systems of linear inequalities, represent them symbolically, and interpret their solutions. Let's start with an example.\n\nSuppose a bakery manager is buying $x$ trays and $y$ boxes for a catering order. The manager needs to purchase at least $36$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]180$. Each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$.\n\nTo model this situation, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- The total number of items is equal to $x+y$. There must be at least $36$ items in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 36$.\n- Since each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$, their total cost is $8x + 2y$. We're told that it cannot exceed $\\[/math]180$. So we have\n$8x + 2y \\leq 180$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system models all possible choices of trays and boxes that both satisfy the minimum item requirement and stay within the budget.\n\n**Watch out!** We consider only non-negative pairs, as the number of items can't be negative." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186218217 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to translate constraints into systems of linear inequalities, represent them symbolically, and interpret their solutions. Let's start with an example.\n\nSuppose a bakery manager is buying $x$ trays and $y$ boxes for a catering order. The manager needs to purchase at least $36$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]180$. Each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$.\n\nTo model this situation, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- The total number of items is equal to $x+y$. There must be at least $36$ items in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 36$.\n- Since each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$, their total cost is $8x + 2y$. We're told that it cannot exceed $\\[/math]180$. So we have\n$8x + 2y \\leq 180$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is the following:\n\n$$\n{x + y \\ge 36; 8x + 2y \\le 180\n$$\n\nThis system models all possible choices of trays and boxes that both satisfy the minimum item requirement and stay within the budget.\n\n**Watch out!** We consider only non-negative pairs, as the number of items can't be negative." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186218218 1064 "For the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nevery pair $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx,y \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthat satisfies both inequalities represents a solution of the system. For example, consider the pair\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (10,40)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into both inequalities, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}10 + 40 \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 ( 10 ) + 2 ( 40 ) \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}50 \\ge  36 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ 80 + 80 = 160 \\le  180 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSince both inequalities are satisfied, the point $(10,40)$ is a solution.\nThus, buying $10$ trays and $40$ boxes is a valid option for the manager in our original problem.\n\nNow, consider\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (10,80)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into both inequalities, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}10 + 80 \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 ( 10 ) + 2 ( 80 ) \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}90 \\ge  36 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ 80 + 160 = 240 \\le  180 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSince the second inequality does not hold, the point $(10,80)$ is *not* a solution.\nThus, buying $10$ trays and $80$ boxes is not a valid option for the manager in our original problem." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186218218 1064 "For the system\n\n$$\n{x + y \\ge 36; 8x + 2y \\le 180\n$$\n\nevery pair $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx,y \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthat satisfies both inequalities represents a solution of the system. For example, consider the pair\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (10,40)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into both inequalities, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n{10 + 40 \\ge 36; 8(10) + 2(40) \\le 180\n$$\n\nSince both inequalities are satisfied, the point $(10,40)$ is a solution.\nThus, buying $10$ trays and $40$ boxes is a valid option for the manager in our original problem.\n\nNow, consider\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (10,80)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into both inequalities, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n{10 + 80 \\ge 36; 8(10) + 2(80) \\le 180\n$$\n\nSince the second inequality does not hold, the point $(10,80)$ is *not* a solution.\nThus, buying $10$ trays and $80$ boxes is not a valid option for the manager in our original problem." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\[math]180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}>} \\hspace{0.222em} 36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n| **Phrasing** | **Symbol** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| at least, no less than | $\\geq$ |\n| at most, no more than | $\\leq$ |\n| more than, greater than | $\\gt$ |\n| less than, fewer than | $\\lt$ |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{x + y \\ge 36; 8x + 2y \\le 180\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\[math]180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x + y>36; 8x + 2y \\le 180\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n| **Phrasing** | **Symbol** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| at least, no less than | $\\geq$ |\n| at most, no more than | $\\leq$ |\n| more than, greater than | $\\gt$ |\n| less than, fewer than | $\\lt$ |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186218220 1064 "Sometimes, a situation has *more than two conditions* that must be satisfied. Let’s return to the bakery manager example from the beginning of the lesson. The manager wanted\n\n- to buy at least $36$ items, and\n- to 