 u(x - 1), h(x) = {1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌊x⌋is even \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}⌊x⌋is odd$\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186229733 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [0, π]$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\nf ( x ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x + 1}} , \\\\[1em]\ng ( x ) = \\tan   x , \\\\[1em]\nh ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ne  1 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{1em} & x = 1.\\end{array} \\right.\n\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186229733 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [0, π]$.\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x + 1}, g(x) = \\tan x, h(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186229734 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = L{f(t)}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n$f ( t ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , \\hspace{1em} & t \\le  2 \\\\ {e}^{- t} \\hspace{1em} & t > 2.\\end{array} \\right.$$F(s) = L{f(t)} =$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186229734 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = L{f(t)}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n$f(t) = {1,\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 2 \\\\ e^{-t}\\end{vmatrix}t > 2$.$F(s) = L{f(t)} =$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186229747 1071 "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by\n\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( t ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t < 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle 1 \\le  t \\le  2 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t > 2 .\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186229747 1071 "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by\n\n$f(t) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 1 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}1 \\le t \\le 2; 0, \\mid t > 2$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186230169 1064 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\[math]180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\[/math]150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\[math]180\\,000 - \\[/math]150\\,000 = \\[math]30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\[/math]180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\[math]150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage error &= \\frac{\\mid estimate - exact value\\begin{vmatrix}}{exact value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 180000 - 150000 \\mid}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 30000\\end{vmatrix}}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{30000}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{15} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186230169 1064 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\[math]180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\[/math]150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\[math]180\\,000 - \\[/math]150\\,000 = \\[math]30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\[/math]180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\[math]150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage error} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{estimate} - \\text{exact value} |}{\\text{exact value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 180 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 - 150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{15}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186230170 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\[/math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\[/math]300{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]300 \\cdot (100% + 35%) &= \\[/math]300 \\cdot 135% \\\\\n&= \\[math]300 \\cdot 1.35 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]405✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186230170 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\[/math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\[/math]300{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 300 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } + 35 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  135 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  1.35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 405 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186230173 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\[/math]228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\[/math]240{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]240 \\cdot (100% - 5%) &= \\[/math]240 \\cdot 95% \\\\\n&= \\[math]240 \\cdot 0.95 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]228✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186230173 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\[/math]228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\[/math]240{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 240 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } - 5 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  95 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  0.95 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 228 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186230174 1064 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\[math]10$ and the new price is $\\[/math]12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\[math]12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\[/math]10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid new value - original value\\begin{vmatrix}}{original value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 12 - 10 \\mid}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2\\end{vmatrix}}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186230174 1064 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\[math]10$ and the new price is $\\[/math]12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\[math]12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\[/math]10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage change} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{new value} - \\text{original value} |}{\\text{original value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 12 - 10 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 2 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231305 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac67$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186231305 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\phantom{ {00}}}$ | $\\dfrac67$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231311 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac23$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186231311 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac23$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\phantom{ {00}}}$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231312 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac16$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac56$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186231312 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac16$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\phantom{ {00}}}$ | $\\dfrac56$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231313 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac49$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac65$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186231313 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac49$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\phantom{ {00}}}$ | $\\dfrac65$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231351 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, to isolate $y$, we need to perform the *opposite of adding*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nto $y$. So, we *subtract*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nfrom $y$, remembering to perform the same operation on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6} + y &= \\frac{11}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{6} + y - \\frac{5}{6} &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, simplifying each side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} \\\\\ny + (\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{5}{6}) &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} \\\\\ny + 0 &= \\frac{6}{6} \\\\\ny &= 6 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186231351 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, to isolate $y$, we need to perform the *opposite of adding*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nto $y$. So, we *subtract*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nfrom $y$, remembering to perform the same operation on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6} + y &= \\frac{11}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{6} + y - \\frac{5}{6} &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, simplifying each side, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle y + {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{11}{6}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ \\displaystyle y + ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{11}{6}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ \\displaystyle y + 0 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{6}} \\\\ \\displaystyle y & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{6}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186231354 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, the variable $a$ is *multiplied* by $9$. To isolate $a$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $9$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9a &= 20 \\\\\n9a \\div 9 &= 20 \\div 9 \\\\\n(9 \\div 9)a &= \\frac{20}{9} \\\\\n1a &= \\frac{20}{9} \\\\\na &= 20 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"