$8b=15$\n\nIn this case, the variable $b$ is *multiplied* by $8$. To isolate $b$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $8$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 8 b & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\\\ \\displaystyle 8 b \\div  8 & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\div  8 \\\\ \\displaystyle ( 8 \\div  8 ) b & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{15}{8}} \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 b & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{15}{8}} \\\\ \\displaystyle b & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{15}{8}}}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186232002 1064 "If the area of a rectangle is known and one side length is given, the missing side length can be found by dividing the area by the known side length. This idea works whether the area, the side length, or both are fractions.\n\nFor example, let's determine the missing side length in the area model below.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac25$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac57$ |\n|  |  |\n\nTo find the missing side length, we divide the area by the length of the known side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25 = \\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions by multiplying the numerators and multiplying the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 5}{7 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing side length is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186232002 1064 "If the area of a rectangle is known and one side length is given, the missing side length can be found by dividing the area by the known side length. This idea works whether the area, the side length, or both are fractions.\n\nFor example, let's determine the missing side length in the area model below.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac25$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\phantom{ {00}}}$ | $\\dfrac57$ |\n|  |  |\n\nTo find the missing side length, we divide the area by the length of the known side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25 = \\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions by multiplying the numerators and multiplying the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 5}{7 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing side length is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186232024 1064 "How can we find the missing side of a rectangle if we know its area and one of its side lengths?\n\n![](images/02/02a6095810d0345cfb7d541df402cd0ae5357ab8fd6cde6f4ca530d93cb6b267.png)\n\nThe *area of a rectangle* is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{A}$ is the area, and $a$ and $b$ are the side lengths.\n\nFrom the figure, we are given:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 96\\,\\mathrm{m}^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 12\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute these values into the area formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n96 &= a \\cdot 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $a$, we divide both sides of the equation by $12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{96}{12} &= \\frac{a \\cdot 12}{12} \\\\\n8 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the missing side is $8\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 false] [17592186232024 1064 "How can we find the missing side of a rectangle if we know its area and one of its side lengths?\n\n![](images/02/02a6095810d0345cfb7d541df402cd0ae5357ab8fd6cde6f4ca530d93cb6b267.png)\n\nThe *area of a rectangle* is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{A}$ is the area, and $a$ and $b$ are the side lengths.\n\nFrom the figure, we are given:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 96\\,\\mathrm{m}^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 12\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute these values into the area formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n96 &= a \\cdot 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $a$, we divide both sides of the equation by $12$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{96}{12}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{a \\cdot  \\cancel{12}}{\\cancel{12}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle 8 & \\displaystyle  = a .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the missing side is $8\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
